{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VU2ZoL4orjLEknqrEQ38SU","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Reviewer Guidelines - Summary\n\nBased on the provided webpage, CoRL offers **limited reviewer guidance**. The document does not include a complete review form, scoring rubric, numerical rating scale, or detailed evaluation dimensions.\n\n## Available Content:\n\n**Review Criteria for Regular Papers:**\nPapers are assessed on \"the significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and \"their potential for scientific and technological impact, as well as their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\n**Scope Requirements:**\nSubmissions must demonstrate robot learning relevance through either:\n- Intent: \"Explicitly address a learning question for physical robots\" OR\n- Outcome: \"Test the proposed learning solution on physical robots\"\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:**\nReviewers should demonstrate expertise through publications, ideally with \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences and journals (e.g. CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, IJRR, TRO, RAL) and core machine learning venues (e.g. NeurIPS, ICML, AIStats, JMLR, PAMI).\"\n\n## Missing Elements:\nThe webpage does not provide:\n- Specific review form questions\n- Numerical scoring scales\n- Detailed evaluation rubrics\n- Quantitative acceptance/rejection thresholds\n\n**Note:** Complete reviewer instructions may be available through the conference's submission platform or reviewer portal."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"9f54644f-f0bc-49e8-985b-01bec20cfa6c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:44:22.824Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":134045,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Reviewer Guidelines - Summary\n\nBased on the provided webpage, CoRL offers **limited reviewer guidance**. The document does not include a complete review form, scoring rubric, numerical rating scale, or detailed evaluation dimensions.\n\n## Available Content:\n\n**Review Criteria for Regular Papers:**\nPapers are assessed on \"the significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and \"their potential for scientific and technological impact, as well as their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\n**Scope Requirements:**\nSubmissions must demonstrate robot learning relevance through either:\n- Intent: \"Explicitly address a learning question for physical robots\" OR\n- Outcome: \"Test the proposed learning solution on physical robots\"\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:**\nReviewers should demonstrate expertise through publications, ideally with \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences and journals (e.g. CoRL, RSS, ICRA, IROS, IJRR, TRO, RAL) and core machine learning venues (e.g. NeurIPS, ICML, AIStats, JMLR, PAMI).\"\n\n## Missing Elements:\nThe webpage does not provide:\n- Specific review form questions\n- Numerical scoring scales\n- Detailed evaluation rubrics\n- Quantitative acceptance/rejection thresholds\n\n**Note:** Complete reviewer instructions may be available through the conference's submission platform or reviewer portal.","durationMs":4144,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VNHbn1gQVzSEZXjvxsgnbE","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Key Evaluation Dimensions\n\nReviewers assess submissions across six main criteria:\n\n1. **Originality**: Whether problems/methods are novel, how the work differs from prior contributions, and adequacy of related work citations.\n\n2. **Quality**: Technical soundness, whether claims are supported by theory or experiments, appropriateness of methods, completeness, and honest evaluation of strengths and weaknesses.\n\n3. **Clarity**: Quality of writing, organization, informativeness, and whether experts could reproduce results from the paper.\n\n4. **Significance**: Importance of results, likelihood of adoption by researchers or practitioners, advancement of state-of-the-art, and unique theoretical or experimental contributions.\n\n5. **Relevance**: Applicability to robot learning, use of learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots, and audience interest.\n\n6. **Limitations**: Appreciation for honestly reported limitations and critical assessment of undisclosed shortcomings.\n\n## Review Structure\n\nThe review form includes:\n- **Summary section**: Restating the paper's contributions in reviewers' own words\n- **Main review**: Evaluating the six dimensions above\n\n## Critical Guidance\n\nThe guidelines emphasize being \"constructive\" and \"concrete\" while avoiding common reviewer mistakes, such as:\n- Rejecting without citing prior work\n- Demanding code/data/real robot validation as strict policies\n- Using SOTA performance alone as acceptance/rejection basis\n- Invalidating papers over minor errors\n\n## Timeline Notes\n\nReviews deadline: July 19, 2023 (23:59 PT)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"b39f6d6c-ba6f-49aa-835a-f33546841db5","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:44:25.094Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Key Evaluation Dimensions\n\nReviewers assess submissions across six main criteria:\n\n1. **Originality**: Whether problems/methods are novel, how the work differs from prior contributions, and adequacy of related work citations.\n\n2. **Quality**: Technical soundness, whether claims are supported by theory or experiments, appropriateness of methods, completeness, and honest evaluation of strengths and weaknesses.\n\n3. **Clarity**: Quality of writing, organization, informativeness, and whether experts could reproduce results from the paper.\n\n4. **Significance**: Importance of results, likelihood of adoption by researchers or practitioners, advancement of state-of-the-art, and unique theoretical or experimental contributions.\n\n5. **Relevance**: Applicability to robot learning, use of learning-based methods, evaluation in simulation or on real robots, and audience interest.\n\n6. **Limitations**: Appreciation for honestly reported limitations and critical assessment of undisclosed shortcomings.\n\n## Review Structure\n\nThe review form includes:\n- **Summary section**: Restating the paper's contributions in reviewers' own words\n- **Main review**: Evaluating the six dimensions above\n\n## Critical Guidance\n\nThe guidelines emphasize being \"constructive\" and \"concrete\" while avoiding common reviewer mistakes, such as:\n- Rejecting without citing prior work\n- Demanding code/data/real robot validation as strict policies\n- Using SOTA performance alone as acceptance/rejection basis\n- Invalidating papers over minor errors\n\n## Timeline Notes\n\nReviews deadline: July 19, 2023 (23:59 PT)","durationMs":5585,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
