{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VGyjdjN9jxfgeFc3yioYib","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for\\\\Robust Vision-Language-Action Models}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{\n16\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n17\t  CoRL 2026 Submission\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%% ============================================================\n26\t%%  ABSTRACT\n27\t%% ============================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) achieve high success rates on standard manipulation benchmarks but degrade sharply under environment perturbations.\n30\tExisting robustness methods either apply perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation or require large parameter budgets for capacity-based expert routing.\n31\tWe observe that frozen VLA internal activations already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class macro-F1 $\\geq 0.99$ at a single layer), yet no existing method exploits this diagnostic signal for targeted correction.\n32\tWe propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a parameter-efficient framework that closes the diagnose--correct loop: a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) classifies the active perturbation type from a single frozen hidden state, then soft-routes corrective signals through a bank of type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual / Spatial / Physical).\n33\tOn LIBERO-Plus with a pi0 backbone, Meta-Brain achieves 71.4\\% overall success with ${\\sim}6$M trainable parameters---an 17.8\\% improvement over the frozen baseline and 6.0\\% over a parameter-matched universal LoRA---capturing 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's absolute robustness gain at 5.3\\% of its parameter cost.\n34\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA replicates the qualitative pattern (+5.6\\% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\n35\tA mechanistic analysis reveals that routing benefit is strongest for perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages (visual, physical) and weakest for those requiring trajectory-level replanning (spatial), providing actionable design guidance for future robustness methods.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%% ============================================================\n39\t%%  1. INTRODUCTION\n40\t%% ============================================================\n41\t\\section{Introduction}\n42\t\n43\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models have achieved remarkable performance on standard manipulation benchmarks, with policies such as pi0 exceeding 90\\% success on LIBERO.\n44\tHowever, these models remain brittle when deployed under environment perturbations: on the recently introduced LIBERO-Plus benchmark, which evaluates robustness across seven perturbation types (camera, lighting, background, noise, distractor objects, layout changes, and robot dynamics), frozen pi0 achieves only 53.6\\%.\n45\t\n46\tExisting approaches to VLA robustness fall into several categories.\n47\t\\emph{Capacity-based routing} methods such as VLA-GSE~\\cite{vla-gse} add Mixture-of-Expert layers that route by per-token capacity needs, achieving 81.2\\% on LIBERO-Plus but requiring 114M trainable parameters.\n48\t\\emph{Visual-only correction} methods such as FTM and FLA achieve strong results on visual perturbations with minimal parameters (4K--4.7M) but cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n49\t\\emph{Uniform adaptation} approaches, including adversarial training (RobustVLA) and universal LoRA, apply the same correction regardless of perturbation type.\n50\tNone of these methods exploit the fact that different perturbation types disrupt different VLA processing stages---visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, and physical perturbations break dynamics assumptions---and thus demand qualitatively different corrections.\n51\t\n52\tWe make three observations that motivate our approach.\n53\tFirst, frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer (PaliGemma layer 6 in pi0) encode perturbation type with near-perfect discriminability (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993), enabling zero-overhead real-time diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n54\tSecond, type-specialized LoRA experts substantially outperform universal LoRA on their target perturbation type (e.g., +13.0\\% for Visual, +9.0\\% for Physical) but \\emph{degrade} performance when applied to the wrong type, making accurate routing critical.\n55\tThird, the optimal layer placement for LoRA correction differs by perturbation type---early layers for visual, mid layers for spatial, late layers for physical---confirming that perturbation types disrupt distinct processing stages.\n56\t\n57\tBased on these observations, we propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a modular framework consisting of:\n58\t(1)~a \\emph{Perturbation-Aware Router} (PAR)---a lightweight MLP classifier (${\\sim}50$K params) operating on frozen layer-6 activations;\n59\t(2)~an \\emph{Adaptive LoRA Bank} (ALB)---three rank-16 LoRA adapters specialized for Visual, Spatial, and Physical perturbations (${\\sim}6$M params total);\n60\tand (3)~an optional \\emph{Correction Memory} (CM) module that modulates routing weights using retrieved exemplars.\n61\tThe PAR produces soft routing weights that blend expert corrections: $\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{corrected}} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_k p(\\text{type}_k) \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}})$.\n62\t\n63\tOur contributions are:\n64\t\\begin{enumerate}\n65\t\\item \\textbf{Empirical finding}: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity, enabling real-time diagnosis without external modules.\n66\t\\item \\textbf{Architecture}: Meta-Brain---a parameter-efficient framework that routes corrective LoRA experts by diagnosed perturbation type, achieving 71.4\\% on LIBERO-Plus with ${\\sim}6$M parameters.\n67\t\\item \\textbf{Design dimension analysis}: Systematic comparison establishing perturbation-diagnostic routing as an orthogonal and composable axis relative to capacity-based routing, universal adaptation, and visual-only correction.\n68\t\\end{enumerate}\n69\t\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t%%  2. RELATED WORK\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{VLA Robustness.}\n76\tLIBERO-Plus introduced a standardized robustness evaluation covering seven perturbation types.\n77\tVLA-GSE adds Mixture-of-Expert layers for capacity routing (114M params, 81.2\\%).\n78\tRobustVLA applies adversarial training for uniform robustness.\n79\tSRPO and CorridorVLA achieve 82.1\\% and 83.2\\% via full-model training.\n80\tThese methods are perturbation-agnostic: the same model configuration handles all perturbation types identically.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Visual Perturbation Correction.}\n83\tFTM corrects visual perturbations with 4K parameters via fine-tuned visual transforms.\n84\tFLA extends this with learned attention-based correction (4.7M params).\n85\tBoth are limited to visual perturbation types and cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Adaptation for VLAs.}\n88\tLoRA and its variants (LoRA-SP, QLoRA) enable efficient fine-tuning.\n89\tTask-specific routing (MoIRA, CORAL) selects adapters by task identity, not perturbation type.\n90\tMeta-Brain introduces perturbation-type routing as a new routing dimension orthogonal to task routing.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Perturbation Diagnosis in VLAs.}\n93\tSAFE demonstrates that VLA internal features can detect out-of-distribution inputs.\n94\tProbing studies on OpenVLA show linear separability of environment factors.\n95\tFeature steering on pi0.5 demonstrates causal influence of internal representations.\n96\tMeta-Brain is the first to close the loop from diagnosis to correction.\n97\t\n98\t%% ============================================================\n99\t%%  3. METHOD\n100\t%% ============================================================\n101\t\\section{Method}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n104\t\n105\tConsider a frozen VLA policy $\\pi_\\theta$ that maps observations $\\mathbf{o}_t$ to actions $\\mathbf{a}_t$.\n106\tUnder a perturbation of type $k \\in \\{\\text{Visual}, \\text{Spatial}, \\text{Physical}\\}$, performance degrades from baseline.\n107\tOur goal is to restore performance via a lightweight, modular adaptation that is aware of the active perturbation type.\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR)}\n110\t\n111\tThe PAR operates on frozen hidden states $\\mathbf{h}^{(\\ell)}$ at layer $\\ell$ of the VLA's vision encoder.\n112\tWe select layer $\\ell=6$ of PaliGemma (pi0's vision backbone) based on probing analysis showing peak perturbation discriminability at this layer (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993).\n113\t\n114\tThe router is a 2-layer MLP with ${\\sim}50$K parameters:\n115\t\\begin{equation}\n116\t  p(k \\mid \\mathbf{o}_t) = \\text{softmax}\\!\\bigl(\\text{MLP}(\\bar{\\mathbf{h}}^{(6)}_t)\\bigr), \\quad k \\in \\{V, S, P\\}\n117\t\\end{equation}\n118\twhere $\\bar{\\mathbf{h}}^{(6)}_t$ is the mean-pooled hidden state across spatial tokens at layer 6.\n119\tPAR is initialized from probing weights (Phase~0) and end-to-end fine-tuned for task success.\n120\t\n121\t\\subsection{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB)}\n122\t\n123\tThe ALB contains three rank-16 LoRA adapters, each specialized for one perturbation coarse type:\n124\t$\\{\\text{LoRA-V}, \\text{LoRA-S}, \\text{LoRA-P}\\}$.\n125\tEach adapter is independently trained on perturbation-type-specific data with frozen pi0 weights.\n126\tAt inference, corrections are blended via soft routing:\n127\t\\begin{equation}\n128\t  \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{corrected}} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_{k \\in \\{V,S,P\\}} p(k \\mid \\mathbf{o}_t) \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}})\n129\t\\end{equation}\n130\t\n131\tDuring routing fine-tuning, LoRA weights are frozen and only PAR parameters are updated.\n132\tThis prevents co-adaptation and ensures that expert specialization is preserved.\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Correction Memory (Optional)}\n135\t\n136\tThe Correction Memory (CM) module maintains a FAISS-indexed memory bank of (observation embedding, routing outcome) pairs from successful episodes.\n137\tAt test time, CM retrieves the $K$ nearest exemplars and produces a modulation signal:\n138\t\\begin{equation}\n139\t  p_{\\text{final}}(k) = (1 - \\alpha) \\cdot p_{\\text{PAR}}(k) + \\alpha \\cdot p_{\\text{CM}}(k)\n140\t\\end{equation}\n141\twhere $\\alpha$ is a single learned parameter.\n142\tCM adds negligible latency (1.4\\,ms) and is presented as an optional enhancement.\n143\t\n144\t%% ============================================================\n145\t%%  4. EXPERIMENTS\n146\t%% ============================================================\n147\t\\section{Experiments}\n148\t\n149\t\\subsection{Setup}\n150\t\n151\t\\paragraph{Backbone.} pi0 with PaliGemma vision encoder, evaluated on LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation types, 50 tasks per type).\n152\t\n153\t\\paragraph{Metrics.} Task success rate (\\%) averaged over 5 seeds.\n154\tStatistical significance via paired $t$-tests.\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Frozen pi0 (0 params), Universal LoRA (rank 16, ${\\sim}2$M params, trained on mixed-type data), Oracle routing (ground-truth perturbation labels), FTM (4K params), FLA (4.7M params), VLA-GSE (114M params, different backbone---Qwen3-VL-4B).\n157\t\n158\t%% ----- 4.2 Expert Specialization -----\n159\t\\subsection{Expert Specialization Analysis}\n160\t\n161\tWe first establish that perturbation-type specialization yields clear benefits by training separate LoRA experts and evaluating each on all perturbation types.\n162\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-type} shows the cross-type performance matrix.\n163\t\n164\t\\begin{table}[h]\n165\t\\centering\n166\t\\caption{Cross-type expert performance (success rate \\%). Specialized experts excel on their target type but degrade below frozen baseline when misapplied.}\n167\t\\label{tab:cross-type}\n168\t\\small\n169\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n170\t\\toprule\n171\tMethod & cam & light & bg & noise & distr & layout & robot & Overall \\\\\n172\t\\midrule\n173\tFrozen pi0 & 47.6 & 56.2 & 51.8 & 49.4 & 58.6 & 57.8 & 53.8 & 53.6 \\\\\n174\tLoRA-V     & 75.2 & 82.0 & 78.4 & 77.6 & 54.2 & 53.8 & 50.6 & 67.4 \\\\\n175\tLoRA-S     & 44.2 & 50.4 & 47.8 & 45.0 & 69.4 & 66.6 & 49.2 & 53.2 \\\\\n176\tLoRA-P     & 45.6 & 51.2 & 48.4 & 46.2 & 54.0 & 53.4 & 78.0 & 53.8 \\\\\n177\tUniversal  & 62.8 & 69.4 & 66.2 & 63.0 & 64.8 & 62.6 & 69.0 & 65.4 \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\tSpecialization deltas (expert own-type minus universal same-type) are +13.0\\% for Visual, +4.3\\% for Spatial, and +9.0\\% for Physical.\n183\tThe asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable: visual perturbations corrupt early PaliGemma representations in a consistent, localized manner amenable to rank-16 LoRA correction.\n184\tPhysical perturbations affect action-space outputs distributed across layers.\n185\tSpatial perturbations (layout, distractor) fundamentally change the required trajectory---not just a representational shift---making single-step LoRA correction insufficient.\n186\t\n187\tCritically, applying the \\emph{wrong} expert degrades performance below the frozen baseline (e.g., LoRA-S on visual types: 46.9\\% vs.\\ frozen 51.3\\%), motivating the need for accurate routing.\n188\t\n189\t%% ----- 4.3 Main Results -----\n190\t\\subsection{Main Results: Ablation Ladder}\n191\t\n192\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation-ladder} presents the core ablation ladder with 5-seed statistics.\n193\tAll adjacent comparisons are statistically significant.\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[h]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\caption{Ablation ladder on LIBERO-Plus (5 seeds). All adjacent $p$-values $< 0.05$.}\n198\t\\label{tab:ablation-ladder}\n199\t\\small\n200\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n201\t\\toprule\n202\tMethod & Params & Mean (\\%) & Std & 95\\% CI & $p$ vs.\\ prev. \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tFrozen pi0      & 0      & 53.6 & 1.8 & [51.4, 55.8] & --- \\\\\n205\tUniversal LoRA  & ${\\sim}$2M  & 65.4 & 2.1 & [62.8, 68.0] & $<0.001$ \\\\\n206\tMeta-Brain      & ${\\sim}$6M  & 71.4 & 2.4 & [68.4, 74.4] & 0.008 \\\\\n207\tOracle          & ${\\sim}$6M  & 75.3 & 1.9 & [72.9, 77.7] & 0.021 \\\\\n208\t\\bottomrule\n209\t\\end{tabular}\n210\t\\end{table}\n211\t\n212\tMeta-Brain improves 6.0 $\\pm$ 2.3\\% over universal LoRA ($p = 0.008$), confirming that routing by perturbation type provides meaningful benefit beyond uniform adaptation.\n213\tThe oracle ceiling (75.3\\%) shows Meta-Brain captures 60.6\\% of the theoretically available routing benefit.\n214\t\n215\tTable~\\ref{tab:per-type} provides the per-perturbation breakdown.\n216\t\n217\t\\begin{table}[h]\n218\t\\centering\n219\t\\caption{Per-perturbation success rates (\\%, 5-seed mean).}\n220\t\\label{tab:per-type}\n221\t\\small\n222\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n223\t\\toprule\n224\tMethod & cam & light & bg & noise & distr & layout & robot & Overall \\\\\n225\t\\midrule\n226\tFrozen     & 47.6 & 56.2 & 51.8 & 49.4 & 58.6 & 57.8 & 53.8 & 53.6 \\\\\n227\tUniversal  & 62.8 & 69.4 & 66.2 & 63.0 & 64.8 & 62.6 & 69.0 & 65.4 \\\\\n228\tMeta-Brain & 71.0 & 77.6 & 74.4 & 73.0 & 65.2 & 64.4 & 74.2 & 71.4 \\\\\n229\tOracle     & 75.2 & 82.0 & 78.4 & 77.6 & 69.4 & 66.6 & 78.0 & 75.3 \\\\\n230\t\\bottomrule\n231\t\\end{tabular}\n232\t\\end{table}\n233\t\n234\tRouting gains are concentrated on visual perturbations (+8.7\\% over universal on average) and physical (+5.2\\%), while spatial gains are marginal (+1.1\\%).\n235\tTable~\\ref{tab:routing-decomposition} decomposes the routing gain by coarse type and reports the fraction of the oracle ceiling captured.\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[h]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Routing gain decomposition by coarse perturbation type.}\n240\t\\label{tab:routing-decomposition}\n241\t\\small\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\tCoarse Type & Universal & Meta-Brain & Routing Gain & \\% of Oracle Ceiling \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\tVisual (4 types)  & 65.4 & 74.0 & +8.7 & 66.8\\% \\\\\n247\tSpatial (2 types)  & 63.7 & 64.8 & +1.1 & 25.6\\% \\\\\n248\tPhysical (1 type) & 69.0 & 74.2 & +5.2 & 57.8\\% \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tOverall           & 65.4 & 71.4 & +6.0 & 60.6\\% \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\t\\paragraph{PAR Routing Analysis.}\n256\tTable~\\ref{tab:routing-weights} shows the mean soft routing weights produced by PAR, grouped by true perturbation type.\n257\tThe near-diagonal structure confirms that PAR correctly identifies perturbation types, with highest confidence on visual inputs (0.82) and lowest on spatial (0.68).\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{table}[h]\n260\t\\centering\n261\t\\caption{PAR soft routing weight matrix (mean weight per true perturbation type).}\n262\t\\label{tab:routing-weights}\n263\t\\small\n264\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n265\t\\toprule\n266\tTrue Type & $p$(LoRA-V) & $p$(LoRA-S) & $p$(LoRA-P) \\\\\n267\t\\midrule\n268\tVisual   & \\textbf{0.82} & 0.06 & 0.12 \\\\\n269\tSpatial  & 0.20 & \\textbf{0.68} & 0.12 \\\\\n270\tPhysical & 0.12 & 0.08 & \\textbf{0.80} \\\\\n271\t\\bottomrule\n272\t\\end{tabular}\n273\t\\end{table}\n274\t\n275\tThe lower spatial diagonality (0.68) explains the reduced routing benefit on spatial perturbations: layout and distractor perturbations share visual features with camera/background changes at layer~6, creating classification ambiguity.\n276\t\n277\t%% ----- 4.4 Comparison with Visual-Only Methods -----\n278\t\\subsection{Comparison with Visual-Only Methods}\n279\t\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:visual-methods} compares Meta-Brain against visual-only baselines (FTM, FLA).\n281\t\n282\t\\begin{table}[h]\n283\t\\centering\n284\t\\caption{Comparison with visual perturbation methods (\\%).}\n285\t\\label{tab:visual-methods}\n286\t\\small\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\tMethod & Params & cam & light & bg & noise & Vis.\\ Avg & Overall \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tFTM        & 4K       & 69.8 & 76.4 & 72.6 & 68.2 & 71.8 & 65.3 \\\\\n292\tFLA        & 4.7M     & 73.8 & 80.2 & 76.8 & 75.4 & 76.6 & 68.1 \\\\\n293\tLoRA-V     & ${\\sim}$2M    & 75.2 & 82.0 & 78.4 & 77.6 & 78.3 & 67.4 \\\\\n294\tMeta-Brain & ${\\sim}$6M    & 71.0 & 77.6 & 74.4 & 73.0 & 74.0 & 71.4 \\\\\n295\tUniversal  & ${\\sim}$2M    & 62.8 & 69.4 & 66.2 & 63.0 & 65.4 & 65.4 \\\\\n296\t\\bottomrule\n297\t\\end{tabular}\n298\t\\end{table}\n299\t\n300\tOn visual perturbations alone, dedicated methods outperform Meta-Brain: LoRA-V (78.3\\%) $>$ FLA (76.6\\%) $>$ Meta-Brain (74.0\\%) $>$ FTM (71.8\\%).\n301\tHowever, visual-only methods cannot address spatial or physical perturbations, resulting in lower overall performance (FTM: 65.3\\%, FLA: 68.1\\% vs.\\ Meta-Brain: 71.4\\%).\n302\tMeta-Brain's value is \\emph{breadth of coverage}, not per-type optimality.\n303\t\n304\t%% ----- 4.5 Parameter Efficiency -----\n305\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency Analysis}\n306\t\n307\tTable~\\ref{tab:param-efficiency} presents a systematic parameter efficiency comparison.\n308\tVLA-GSE results use a different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B); we compare gain-over-respective-frozen-baseline for fair evaluation.\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[h]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency comparison. $\\dagger$Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B).}\n313\t\\label{tab:param-efficiency}\n314\t\\small\n315\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n316\t\\toprule\n317\tMethod & Backbone & Params (M) & Gain (\\%) & Gain/M & Coverage \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tFTM            & pi0          & 0.004 & 11.7 & 2925.0 & Visual only \\\\\n320\tUniversal LoRA & pi0          & 2     & 11.8 & 5.9    & All types \\\\\n321\tFLA            & pi0          & 4.7   & 14.5 & 3.1    & Visual only \\\\\n322\tMeta-Brain     & pi0          & 6     & 17.8 & 3.0    & All types \\\\\n323\tVLA-GSE$\\dagger$ & Qwen3-VL-4B & 114   & 27.6 & 0.24   & All types \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\tMeta-Brain achieves 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's absolute robustness gain using 5.3\\% of its trainable parameters.\n329\tAmong multi-type methods, Meta-Brain extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA: routing-based specialization extracts more absolute gain per parameter than simply increasing uniform LoRA rank.\n330\tFTM is an extreme outlier in per-parameter efficiency (2925\\%/M) but is limited to a single perturbation modality.\n331\t\n332\t%% ----- 4.6 Mechanistic Analysis -----\n333\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Analysis: Why Routing Benefits Vary by Type}\n334\t\n335\tThe asymmetric routing benefit across perturbation types (visual: +8.7\\%, physical: +5.2\\%, spatial: +1.1\\%) demands explanation.\n336\tWe present two analyses that illuminate this pattern.\n337\t\n338\t\\paragraph{LoRA Layer Placement Ablation.}\n339\tTable~\\ref{tab:layer-placement} shows expert performance when LoRA adapters are restricted to different layer ranges of PaliGemma.\n340\t\n341\t\\begin{table}[h]\n342\t\\centering\n343\t\\caption{Expert performance (\\%) by LoRA layer placement (own-type evaluation).}\n344\t\\label{tab:layer-placement}\n345\t\\small\n346\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n347\t\\toprule\n348\tLayer Range & LoRA-V (Visual) & LoRA-S (Spatial) & LoRA-P (Physical) \\\\\n349\t\\midrule\n350\tEarly (2--6)   & \\textbf{80.2} & 64.6 & 72.8 \\\\\n351\tMid (6--12)    & 74.8 & \\textbf{70.2} & 75.2 \\\\\n352\tLate (12--16)  & 71.4 & 66.8 & \\textbf{80.6} \\\\\n353\tAll layers     & 78.3 & 68.0 & 78.0 \\\\\n354\t\\bottomrule\n355\t\\end{tabular}\n356\t\\end{table}\n357\t\n358\tEach expert type peaks at a different processing stage: LoRA-V at early layers (where visual encoding occurs), LoRA-S at mid layers (where spatial reasoning integrates multi-token context), and LoRA-P at late layers (where action predictions implicitly model dynamics).\n359\tThis crossed-peak pattern confirms that perturbation types disrupt distinct processing stages, providing the mechanistic basis for type-specific routing.\n360\t\n361\tCombining type-optimal layer placements yields 73.8\\% overall (+2.4\\% over uniform all-layer placement at 71.4\\%), suggesting layer-aware routing as a promising future extension.\n362\t\n363\t\\begin{table}[h]\n364\t\\centering\n365\t\\caption{Optimal layer placement per expert type.}\n366\t\\label{tab:layer-optimal}\n367\t\\small\n368\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n369\t\\toprule\n370\tExpert & Best Layers & Own-Type Success & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ All-Layer \\\\\n371\t\\midrule\n372\tLoRA-V & Early (2--6) & 80.2 & +1.9 \\\\\n373\tLoRA-S & Mid (6--12) & 70.2 & +2.2 \\\\\n374\tLoRA-P & Late (12--16) & 80.6 & +2.6 \\\\\n375\t\\bottomrule\n376\t\\end{tabular}\n377\t\\end{table}\n378\t\n379\t\\paragraph{Spatial Underperformance: A Fundamental Limitation.}\n380\tThe spatial expert's weak specialization (+4.3\\% over universal) and low routing diagonality (0.68) are not routing failures but reflect a fundamental limitation.\n381\tLayout and distractor perturbations change the \\emph{goal-relevant spatial configuration}, requiring trajectory-level replanning that single-step representation correction cannot provide.\n382\tLoRA operates at the representation level---it can invert a corrupted encoding but cannot replan a trajectory.\n383\tThis interpretation is supported by three converging observations:\n384\t(1)~the spatial specialization delta is consistently smallest across both pi0 and SmolVLA backbones;\n385\t(2)~mid-layer placement improves spatial correction because spatial reasoning occurs at mid layers, yet the improvement remains modest;\n386\t(3)~even oracle routing only raises spatial performance to 68.0\\% (vs.\\ 78.3\\% and 78.0\\% for visual and physical respectively), confirming a ceiling effect inherent to LoRA-based spatial correction.\n387\t\n388\t%% ----- 4.7 Ablation Studies -----\n389\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n390\t\n391\t\\paragraph{Correction Memory.}\n392\tTable~\\ref{tab:cm-ablation} shows the marginal contribution of the Correction Memory module.\n393\tCM provides +1.2\\% overall, concentrated on physical perturbations (+2.8\\%) where consistent robot dynamics changes allow exemplar retrieval to sharpen routing.\n394\tVisual gain is modest (+0.8\\%) since PAR already routes visual types confidently, and spatial gain is minimal (+0.4\\%) since spatial perturbations vary combinatorially across episodes.\n395\tThe learned blending weight $\\alpha = 0.23$ confirms CM acts as a secondary correction.\n396\t\n397\t\\begin{table}[h]\n398\t\\centering\n399\t\\caption{Correction Memory ablation (\\%).}\n400\t\\label{tab:cm-ablation}\n401\t\\small\n402\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n403\t\\toprule\n404\tConfiguration & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall \\\\\n405\t\\midrule\n406\tMeta-Brain (no CM) & 74.0 & 64.8 & 74.2 & 71.4 \\\\\n407\tMeta-Brain + CM    & 74.8 & 65.2 & 77.0 & 72.6 \\\\\n408\tCM Delta           & +0.8 & +0.4 & +2.8 & +1.2 \\\\\n409\t\\bottomrule\n410\t\\end{tabular}\n411\t\\end{table}\n412\t\n413\t\\paragraph{Router Architecture.}\n414\tTable~\\ref{tab:router-arch} compares PAR router variants.\n415\tThe 2-layer MLP (${\\sim}50$K params) provides the best cost/performance tradeoff.\n416\tA linear classifier loses 1.2\\% task success despite only 3.2\\% lower routing accuracy, confirming that routing accuracy matters at the margins.\n417\tA cross-attention router adds 0.4\\% routing accuracy but this does not translate to task success improvement, indicating that perturbation type classification at layer~6 is a simple problem where attention over token sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling.\n418\t\n419\t\\begin{table}[h]\n420\t\\centering\n421\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison.}\n422\t\\label{tab:router-arch}\n423\t\\small\n424\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n425\t\\toprule\n426\tRouter & Params & Routing Acc.\\ (\\%) & Task Success (\\%) & Overhead (ms) \\\\\n427\t\\midrule\n428\tLinear         & 6K   & 90.6 & 70.2 & 0.02 \\\\\n429\tMLP (default)  & 50K  & 93.8 & 71.4 & 0.05 \\\\\n430\tCross-Attention & 120K & 94.2 & 71.2 & 0.18 \\\\\n431\t\\bottomrule\n432\t\\end{tabular}\n433\t\\end{table}\n434\t\n435\t\\paragraph{6-Class vs.\\ 3-Class Routing.}\n436\tTable~\\ref{tab:granularity} tests whether finer-grained routing justifies additional parameters.\n437\t6-class routing (one expert per perturbation type, ${\\sim}12$M params) yields only +0.8\\% over 3-class (${\\sim}6$M) while doubling the parameter budget and dropping routing accuracy by 4.4\\%.\n438\tThe marginal gain arises primarily from a dedicated robot expert (+7.4\\% on robot type), while intra-Visual types do not benefit from separate experts because they share the same early-representation failure mode.\n439\t\n440\t\\begin{table}[h]\n441\t\\centering\n442\t\\caption{6-class vs.\\ 3-class routing comparison.}\n443\t\\label{tab:granularity}\n444\t\\small\n445\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n446\t\\toprule\n447\tConfig & Classes & Params & Routing Acc.\\ (\\%) & Overall (\\%) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n448\t\\midrule\n449\t3-class Meta-Brain & 3 & ${\\sim}$6M  & 93.8 & 71.4 & --- \\\\\n450\t6-class Meta-Brain & 6 & ${\\sim}$12M & 89.4 & 72.2 & +0.8 \\\\\n451\t\\bottomrule\n452\t\\end{tabular}\n453\t\\end{table}\n454\t\n455\t\\begin{table}[h]\n456\t\\centering\n457\t\\caption{6-class per-type breakdown vs.\\ 3-class (\\%).}\n458\t\\label{tab:6class-detail}\n459\t\\small\n460\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n461\t\\toprule\n462\tPerturbation & 3-class & 6-class & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n463\t\\midrule\n464\tcamera     & 71.0 & 72.8 & +1.8 \\\\\n465\tlight      & 77.6 & 79.2 & +1.6 \\\\\n466\tbackground & 74.4 & 76.0 & +1.6 \\\\\n467\tnoise      & 73.0 & 74.6 & +1.6 \\\\\n468\tdistractor & 65.2 & 66.0 & +0.8 \\\\\n469\tlayout     & 64.4 & 65.2 & +0.8 \\\\\n470\trobot      & 74.2 & 81.6 & +7.4 \\\\\n471\t\\bottomrule\n472\t\\end{tabular}\n473\t\\end{table}\n474\t\n475\tThe 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages (early/mid/late) and provides the better parameter-efficiency tradeoff.\n476\t\n477\t%% ----- 4.8 Cross-Backbone Validation -----\n478\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n479\t\n480\tTo test generalizability beyond pi0, we replicate the full pipeline on SmolVLA, which uses a different vision encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM-360M).\n481\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-backbone} compares key metrics.\n482\t\n483\t\\begin{table}[h]\n484\t\\centering\n485\t\\caption{Cross-backbone comparison (pi0 vs.\\ SmolVLA).}\n486\t\\label{tab:cross-backbone}\n487\t\\small\n488\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n489\t\\toprule\n490\tMetric & pi0 & SmolVLA \\\\\n491\t\\midrule\n492\tPhase 0 3-class F1          & 0.993 & 0.938 \\\\\n493\tBest classification layer   & 6     & 4 \\\\\n494\tFrozen baseline (\\%)        & 53.6  & 46.2 \\\\\n495\tUniversal LoRA (\\%)         & 65.4  & 56.8 \\\\\n496\tMeta-Brain (\\%)             & 71.4  & 62.4 \\\\\n497\tGain over frozen (\\%)       & 17.8  & 16.2 \\\\\n498\tGain over universal (\\%)    & 6.0   & 5.6 \\\\\n499\tRouting diagonality         & 0.77  & 0.73 \\\\\n500\tVisual spec.\\ delta (\\%)   & 13.0  & 10.4 \\\\\n501\tSpatial spec.\\ delta (\\%)  & 4.3   & 2.8 \\\\\n502\t\\bottomrule\n503\t\\end{tabular}\n504\t\\end{table}\n505\t\n506\tThe qualitative pattern replicates exactly: same per-type specialization ranking (Visual $>$ Physical $>$ Spatial), same ablation ladder ordering, and comparable relative gains (+5.6\\% vs.\\ +6.0\\% over universal).\n507\tLower absolute numbers reflect SmolVLA's weaker backbone capacity.\n508\tThe spatial underperformance persists across architectures, confirming it is a property of the perturbation type, not the backbone.\n509\t\n510\t%% ============================================================\n511\t%%  5. DISCUSSION\n512\t%% ============================================================\n513\t\\section{Discussion}\n514\t\n515\t\\paragraph{Meta-Brain as a Complement to Capacity Routing.}\n516\tMeta-Brain and VLA-GSE operate in fundamentally different regions of the parameter-efficiency space.\n517\tMeta-Brain achieves 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3\\% of its parameter cost, suggesting these approaches are complementary rather than competing.\n518\tAn ideal system might compose both: Meta-Brain for type-aware correction at the representation level, and capacity routing for residual errors requiring additional model capacity.\n519\t\n520\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n521\t(1)~Spatial perturbation handling remains weak, limited by LoRA's inability to replan trajectories.\n522\t(2)~Evaluation is simulation-only (LIBERO-Plus), though this is standard for VLA architecture contributions.\n523\t(3)~The 3-class taxonomy assumes perturbation types are known a priori; compound perturbations remain untested.\n524\t(4)~Cross-backbone validation on SmolVLA shows somewhat lower routing benefit, and additional architectures would strengthen generalizability claims.\n525\t\n526\t%% ============================================================\n527\t%%  6. CONCLUSION\n528\t%% ============================================================\n529\t\\section{Conclusion}\n530\t\n531\tWe presented Meta-Brain, a parameter-efficient framework that routes corrective LoRA experts by perturbation type diagnosed from frozen VLA activations.\n532\tOn LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 71.4\\% with ${\\sim}6$M trainable parameters, improving 17.8\\% over frozen pi0 and 6.0\\% over universal LoRA ($p = 0.008$).\n533\tThe ablation ladder Frozen $<$ Universal $<$ Meta-Brain $<$ Oracle is clean and statistically significant across all steps.\n534\tMechanistic analysis reveals that routing benefit correlates with perturbation locality: strongest for visual perturbations that disrupt early representations, moderate for physical perturbations affecting late-layer dynamics, and weakest for spatial perturbations requiring trajectory-level replanning beyond LoRA's representational correction.\n535\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA confirms perturbation-diagnostic routing as an architecture-independent principle.\n536\t\n537\tThese results establish perturbation-type-aware routing as a new, orthogonal design dimension for VLA robustness---one that is parameter-efficient, mechanistically grounded, and composable with existing approaches.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n540\t\n541\t\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n542\t\n543\t\\bibitem{vla-gse}\n544\tVLA-GSE: Generalized Skill Enhancement for Vision-Language-Action Models via Mixture-of-Experts.\n545\t\\textit{CVPR}, 2026.\n546\t\n547\t\\bibitem{libero-plus}\n548\tLIBERO-Plus: A Robustness Benchmark for Vision-Language-Action Models.\n549\t\\textit{CVPR}, 2026.\n550\t\n551\t\\bibitem{pi0}\n552\tpi0: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control.\n553\t\\textit{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem{safe}\n556\tSAFE: Saliency-Aware Failure Explanation for VLA Models.\n557\t\\textit{ICRA}, 2026.\n558\t\n559\t\\bibitem{ftm}\n560\tFine-Tuned Visual Transforms for Robust Robot Manipulation.\n561\t\\textit{CoRL}, 2025.\n562\t\n563\t\\bibitem{lora}\n564\tHu et al. LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.\n565\t\\textit{ICLR}, 2022.\n566\t\n567\t\\bibitem{robustvla}\n568\tRobustVLA: Robustness Enhancement for Vision-Language-Action Models via Adversarial Training.\n569\t2025.\n570\t\n571\t\\bibitem{smolvla}\n572\tSmolVLA: A Small Vision-Language-Action Model for Efficient Robot Learning.\n573\t2025.\n574\t\n575\t\\bibitem{srpo}\n576\tSRPO: Self-Refined Policy Optimization for Robust VLA.\n577\t2026.\n578\t\n579\t\\end{thebibliography}\n580\t\n581\t\\end{document}\n582\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgS46ueuGs5A8Jd8wKpTUy","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"b2e30bfc-ea05-4146-9784-e35bef7e867e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:45:44.651Z"}
