{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FsJmgN9TK7Z16L9hGvnmtD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CpcgGxxwUSUkbUsHPRB6Xc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/median/review_report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3185,"cache_read_input_tokens":24221,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3185,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDJwwiziRzCzZJENTqR7y4","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"ab0b6adf-be68-4118-8449-03ada68ed285"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AjDYiayDi3QJfAmpccun4e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n4\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n12\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{Meta-Brain}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\libeplus}{LIBERO-Plus}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong?\\\\Probing Perturbation Representations and\\\\the Limits of Diagnostic Routing}\n18\t\n19\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n20\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n21\t\n22\t\\date{CoRL 2026 Submission}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t%=============================================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t%=============================================================================\n30\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) suffer severe performance degradation under environmental perturbations, yet it remains unknown whether their internal representations encode information about \\emph{what type} of perturbation occurred.\n31\tWe present two findings.\n32\t\\textbf{First}, frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy: a linear probe on PaliGemma layer~6 of pi0 achieves macro-F1$=$1.0 at $K{=}1$ (one observation step), and this result generalizes to SmolVLA (F1$=$0.971).\n33\tThis establishes \\emph{perturbation diagnosability} as a general property of VLA representations.\n34\t\\textbf{Second}, we test whether this diagnostic signal can be exploited for targeted correction via \\emph{perturbation-diagnostic routing}: a lightweight router directs inference through type-specialized LoRA experts instead of a single universal adapter.\n35\tOn \\libeplus{}, the routed system (\\ours{}, 6M params) achieves 66.3\\% overall success, a +12.7\\% gain over the frozen baseline but only +1.5\\% over a universal LoRA (64.8\\%), a difference that is \\emph{not statistically significant} ($p{=}0.12$, 5-seed paired $t$-test).\n36\tOracle routing with ground-truth labels yields 67.8\\%, capping the routing benefit at 3.0\\%.\n37\tWe trace the bottleneck to expert capacity: at rank~16, specialized LoRA adapters converge to similar corrections regardless of perturbation type, leaving little margin for routing to exploit.\n38\tOur results map the design landscape for perturbation-aware VLA adaptation and identify expert capacity---not routing quality---as the binding constraint.\n39\t\\end{abstract}\n40\t\n41\t%=============================================================================\n42\t\\section{Introduction}\n43\t\\label{sec:intro}\n44\t%=============================================================================\n45\t\n46\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) such as pi0~\\cite{black2024pi0} and OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} achieve high success rates on standard manipulation benchmarks, but their performance drops sharply under environmental perturbations.\n47\tOn \\libeplus{}~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus}, a robustness benchmark spanning six perturbation categories, a frozen pi0 backbone achieves only 53.6\\% overall success---a collapse of roughly 40 points relative to clean conditions.\n48\t\n49\tExisting robustness methods address this gap through diverse strategies: feature transformation modules (FTM)~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} target visual perturbations with remarkable parameter efficiency ($\\sim$4K params); VLA-GSE~\\cite{qu2025vlags} routes tokens through capacity-based Mixture-of-Experts with 114M trainable parameters; RobustVLA~\\cite{wang2025robustvla} applies perturbation-agnostic adversarial training.\n50\tNone of these methods ask a fundamental representational question: \\emph{do VLA internal activations encode what type of perturbation is present?}\n51\t\n52\tWe answer this question affirmatively.\n53\tBy training linear probes on frozen intermediate-layer activations of pi0's PaliGemma vision encoder, we find that perturbation type (Visual / Spatial / Physical) is diagnosable with macro-F1$\\geq$0.99 from a single layer (layer~6) at the very first observation step ($K{=}1$).\n54\tThis finding replicates on SmolVLA (F1$=$0.971 at layer~4), suggesting that \\emph{perturbation diagnosability} is a general property of VLA representations rather than a backbone-specific artifact.\n55\t\n56\tGiven that the diagnostic signal is strong, a natural hypothesis follows: routing corrective adaptation through type-specialized experts should outperform perturbation-agnostic universal adaptation.\n57\tWe test this hypothesis with \\ours{}, a modular architecture that combines a Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) with a bank of type-specialized LoRA adapters.\n58\tThe result is a \\emph{systematic negative finding}: while \\ours{} improves over the frozen baseline by +12.7\\%, its marginal gain over a universal LoRA trained on mixed perturbation data is only +1.5\\%, which is not statistically significant across five evaluation seeds ($p{=}0.12$).\n59\tAn oracle ablation with ground-truth perturbation labels caps the maximum routing benefit at +3.0\\%.\n60\t\n61\tWe trace the bottleneck to \\emph{expert capacity}: at rank~16, LoRA adapters lack sufficient parameters to learn meaningfully different type-specific corrections.\n62\tThe maximum specialization advantage of any expert over the universal adapter is only +4.0 percentage points.\n63\tA universal LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data, implicitly learns perturbation-conditional corrections that capture most of the specialization benefit.\n64\t\n65\tOur contributions are:\n66\t\\begin{enumerate}\n67\t    \\item \\textbf{Perturbation diagnosability.} We show that frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity across two architectures (pi0 and SmolVLA), enabling zero-overhead real-time diagnosis.\n68\t    \\item \\textbf{Informative negative result.} We demonstrate that explicit perturbation-diagnostic routing yields only marginal, statistically non-significant gains over implicit mixed-perturbation LoRA training at rank~16, providing evidence that the universal adapter already captures perturbation-conditional structure.\n69\t    \\item \\textbf{Design-dimension analysis.} We systematically map the landscape of perturbation-aware adaptation---varying routing granularity, router architecture, layer placement, expert capacity, and correction memory---identifying expert capacity as the binding constraint and charting directions for future work.\n70\t\\end{enumerate}\n71\t\n72\t%=============================================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\\label{sec:related}\n75\t%=============================================================================\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{VLA robustness.}\n78\t\\libeplus{}~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} introduced a standardized perturbation benchmark with six categories and proposed Feature Transformation Modules (FTM) for visual robustness.\n79\tVLA-GSE~\\cite{qu2025vlags} applies Mixture-of-Experts routing based on per-token capacity needs, achieving 81.2\\% with 114M trainable parameters on a Qwen3-VL backbone.\n80\tRobustVLA~\\cite{wang2025robustvla} improves robustness via adversarial training.\n81\tSAFE~\\cite{zhang2025safe} detects perturbations from internal representations but does not close the loop to type-specific correction.\n82\tOur work bridges diagnosis and correction, but finds that the correction bottleneck---not the diagnosis---limits downstream gains.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{LoRA and parameter-efficient adaptation.}\n85\tLow-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\\cite{hu2022lora} has become the dominant approach for parameter-efficient fine-tuning.\n86\tRecent work applies LoRA to VLAs for robustness~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} and task specialization.\n87\tMoIRA and CORAL explore task-conditioned LoRA routing.\n88\tWe contribute to this line by examining \\emph{perturbation-conditioned} routing and providing evidence that, at low ranks, mixed training subsumes the benefits of specialized experts.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Mixture-of-Experts routing.}\n91\tMoE architectures route inputs to specialized sub-networks~\\cite{shazeer2017outrageously,fedus2022switch}.\n92\tIn VLAs, VLA-GSE~\\cite{qu2025vlags} routes by per-token capacity.\n93\tOur PAR router instead routes by diagnosed perturbation type---a semantically grounded routing signal.\n94\tWe find this signal is accurate but yields limited downstream benefit under low expert capacity.\n95\t\n96\t%=============================================================================\n97\t\\section{Method}\n98\t\\label{sec:method}\n99\t%=============================================================================\n100\t\n101\t\\subsection{Perturbation Taxonomy}\n102\t\\label{sec:taxonomy}\n103\t\n104\tWe group the six \\libeplus{} perturbation categories into three functional classes based on which VLA processing stage they primarily disrupt:\n105\t\\begin{itemize}\n106\t    \\item \\textbf{Visual} (V): camera pose, lighting, background, sensor noise---corrupt early visual features.\n107\t    \\item \\textbf{Spatial} (S): table layout---invalidate spatial reasoning and trajectory planning.\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{Physical} (P): robot appearance---disrupt embodiment-dependent force and kinematics assumptions.\n109\t\\end{itemize}\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Phase 0: Perturbation-Aware Probing}\n112\t\\label{sec:phase0}\n113\t\n114\tGiven a frozen VLA backbone $f_\\theta$, we extract intermediate activations $\\mathbf{h}_l \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ at layer $l$ from the vision encoder during a single forward pass on a perturbed observation.\n115\tWe train a linear probe $g_\\phi: \\mathbb{R}^d \\to \\{V, S, P\\}$ to classify perturbation type from $\\mathbf{h}_l$.\n116\t\n117\tFor pi0 (PaliGemma vision encoder, 18 layers), we sweep all layers and find that layer~6 achieves 3-class macro-F1$=$0.993 and binary (perturbed vs.\\ clean) AUC$=$1.0.\n118\tAt $K{=}1$ (single observation step), the F1 reaches 1.0---perturbation type is perfectly diagnosable from one frame.\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR)}\n121\t\\label{sec:par}\n122\t\n123\tThe PAR router is a lightweight MLP that maps layer-$l$ activations to a soft routing distribution over $N$ expert classes:\n124\t\\begin{equation}\n125\t    \\mathbf{w} = \\text{softmax}(\\text{MLP}(\\mathbf{h}_l)) \\in \\Delta^{N-1}\n126\t\\end{equation}\n127\tFor $N{=}3$ (V/S/P), this produces a probability vector used to combine expert outputs via weighted averaging.\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB)}\n130\t\\label{sec:alb}\n131\t\n132\tWe train $N$ LoRA adapters $\\{\\Delta W_i\\}_{i=1}^N$, each of rank $r$, on the frozen backbone.\n133\tEach expert is trained on data from its corresponding perturbation class.\n134\tA universal LoRA adapter, trained on all perturbation types mixed, serves as the primary baseline.\n135\t\n136\tAt inference, the router-weighted correction is:\n137\t\\begin{equation}\n138\t    \\Delta W_{\\text{routed}} = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i \\cdot \\Delta W_i\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\t\n141\tUnder our default configuration ($N{=}3$, $r{=}16$), each expert has $\\sim$2M parameters, totaling $\\sim$6M for the full bank.\n142\t\n143\t\\subsection{Correction Memory (CM)}\n144\t\\label{sec:cm}\n145\t\n146\tAn optional module that modulates routing weights using retrieved episodic exemplars.\n147\tA FAISS index stores (activation, perturbation-type, routing-weight) tuples from training episodes.\n148\tAt inference, $k$-nearest neighbors are retrieved, and their routing weights are blended with the PAR output via a learned scalar $\\alpha$:\n149\t\\begin{equation}\n150\t    \\mathbf{w}_{\\text{final}} = (1-\\alpha)\\,\\mathbf{w}_{\\text{PAR}} + \\alpha\\,\\mathbf{w}_{\\text{retrieved}}\n151\t\\end{equation}\n152\t\n153\t\\subsection{Full \\ours{} Pipeline}\n154\t\\label{sec:pipeline}\n155\t\n156\tThe complete system operates as: (1) a perturbed observation passes through the frozen backbone; (2) layer-$l$ activations are extracted; (3) the PAR router produces soft routing weights; (4) expert LoRA outputs are combined according to these weights; (5) the corrected representation is decoded into actions.\n157\tThe router adds negligible latency ($<$1ms) since it reuses activations from the standard forward pass.\n158\t\n159\t%=============================================================================\n160\t\\section{Experiments}\n161\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n162\t%=============================================================================\n163\t\n164\t\\subsection{Setup}\n165\t\\label{sec:setup}\n166\t\n167\t\\paragraph{Backbone.} Pi0~\\cite{black2024pi0} with frozen PaliGemma vision encoder (18 layers, 224M parameters).\n168\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA.\n169\t\n170\t\\paragraph{Benchmark.} \\libeplus{}~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} with six perturbation categories: camera pose, lighting change, background swap, sensor noise, table layout, and robot appearance.\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{LoRA configuration.} Rank 16, applied to all attention layers of PaliGemma.\n173\tThree specialized experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P) and one universal expert trained on mixed data.\n174\t\n175\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.} Success rate (\\%) averaged over 5 random seeds.\n176\tStatistical significance via paired $t$-test.\n177\tWe report per-perturbation-type and overall success rates.\n178\t\n179\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n180\t\\subsection{Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosability}\n181\t\\label{sec:exp_phase0}\n182\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n183\t\n184\tTable~\\ref{tab:phase0} summarizes the probing results across backbones.\n185\tOn pi0, a linear probe at PaliGemma layer~6 achieves 3-class macro-F1$=$0.993 and perfect F1$=$1.0 at $K{=}1$.\n186\tOn SmolVLA, layer~4 yields F1$=$0.971, with $K{=}1$ F1$=$0.943.\n187\tBoth backbones achieve binary AUC$\\geq$0.998.\n188\t\n189\tThis establishes that perturbation type information is readily available in frozen VLA representations---a strong, generalizable positive finding.\n190\t\n191\t\\begin{table}[h]\n192\t\\centering\n193\t\\caption{Phase 0: Perturbation diagnosability across backbones.}\n194\t\\label{tab:phase0}\n195\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n196\t\\toprule\n197\tMetric & pi0 (PaliGemma) & SmolVLA \\\\\n198\t\\midrule\n199\t3-class macro-F1 & 0.993 & 0.971 \\\\\n200\tBest layer & 6 & 4 \\\\\n201\t$K{=}1$ F1 & 1.000 & 0.943 \\\\\n202\tBinary AUC & 1.000 & 0.998 \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\end{table}\n206\t\n207\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n208\t\\subsection{LoRA Expert Training}\n209\t\\label{sec:exp_experts}\n210\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n211\t\n212\tWe train three specialized LoRA experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P) and one universal LoRA on the frozen pi0 backbone.\n213\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_eval} shows the full cross-evaluation matrix.\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[h]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\caption{Cross-evaluation: Expert $\\times$ Perturbation type (success rate \\%).\n218\tBold$=$expert matched to own perturbation type.}\n219\t\\label{tab:cross_eval}\n220\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n221\t\\toprule\n222\tExpert & Camera & Light & BG & Noise & Layout & Robot & Avg \\\\\n223\t\\midrule\n224\tLoRA-V & \\textbf{61.0} & \\textbf{70.0} & \\textbf{65.0} & \\textbf{72.0} & 58.0 & 65.0 & 65.2 \\\\\n225\tLoRA-S & 53.0 & 63.0 & 57.0 & 65.0 & \\textbf{66.0} & 67.0 & 61.8 \\\\\n226\tLoRA-P & 54.0 & 64.0 & 58.0 & 66.0 & 60.0 & \\textbf{73.0} & 62.5 \\\\\n227\tUniversal & 57.0 & 68.0 & 61.0 & 70.0 & 62.0 & 70.8 & 64.8 \\\\\n228\tFrozen pi0 & 44.0 & 56.0 & 49.0 & 58.0 & 52.0 & 62.6 & 53.6 \\\\\n229\t\\bottomrule\n230\t\\end{tabular}\n231\t\\end{table}\n232\t\n233\tThe specialization advantage is modest: matched experts outperform the universal adapter by at most +4.0 percentage points (Table~\\ref{tab:spec_delta}).\n234\tMismatched experts often \\emph{underperform} the universal adapter (e.g., LoRA-V on layout: 58.0 vs.\\ 62.0), indicating that routing must be accurate merely to avoid degradation.\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{table}[h]\n237\t\\centering\n238\t\\caption{Specialization delta: matched expert minus universal LoRA.}\n239\t\\label{tab:spec_delta}\n240\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n241\t\\toprule\n242\tType & Specialized & Universal & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n243\t\\midrule\n244\tCamera & 61.0 & 57.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n245\tLight & 70.0 & 68.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n246\tBackground & 65.0 & 61.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n247\tNoise & 72.0 & 70.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n248\tLayout & 66.0 & 62.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n249\tRobot & 73.0 & 70.8 & +2.2 \\\\\n250\t\\bottomrule\n251\t\\end{tabular}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\tThe universal LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data, achieves 64.8\\% overall---only 3.0\\% below the best possible matched-expert assignment (67.8\\% under oracle routing).\n255\tThis suggests that mixed training acts as implicit multi-task learning, allowing a single rank-16 adapter to learn perturbation-conditional corrections without any routing signal.\n256\t\n257\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n258\t\\subsection{\\ours{} Assembly and Evaluation}\n259\t\\label{sec:exp_metabrain}\n260\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n261\t\n262\tWe assemble the full \\ours{} system: PAR router (2-layer MLP on PaliGemma layer~6) with three LoRA experts under soft routing.\n263\tTable~\\ref{tab:metabrain} shows the per-perturbation results.\n264\t\n265\t\\begin{table}[h]\n266\t\\centering\n267\t\\caption{\\ours{} per-perturbation results compared to baselines.}\n268\t\\label{tab:metabrain}\n269\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc|c|c}\n270\t\\toprule\n271\tMethod & Camera & Light & BG & Noise & Layout & Robot & Overall & $\\Delta$(MB$-$Uni) \\\\\n272\t\\midrule\n273\tFrozen pi0 & 44.0 & 56.0 & 49.0 & 58.0 & 52.0 & 62.6 & 53.6 & --- \\\\\n274\tUniversal LoRA & 57.0 & 68.0 & 61.0 & 70.0 & 62.0 & 70.8 & 64.8 & --- \\\\\n275\t\\ours{} & 59.0 & 69.0 & 63.0 & 71.0 & 64.0 & 72.0 & 66.3 & +1.5 \\\\\n276\tOracle routing & 61.0 & 70.0 & 65.0 & 72.0 & 66.0 & 73.0 & 67.8 & +3.0 \\\\\n277\t\\bottomrule\n278\t\\end{tabular}\n279\t\\end{table}\n280\t\n281\t\\ours{} achieves 66.3\\% overall (+12.7\\% over frozen), but only +1.5\\% over the universal LoRA.\n282\tThe routing weight matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:routing_weights}) reveals weakly diagonal routing (diagonality$=$0.58; perfect$=$1.0, uniform$=$0.33) with mean entropy of 0.89 (near-uniform$=$1.10 for 3 classes).\n283\tThe router assigns some preference to the matched expert but distributes substantial weight to the others.\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[h]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\caption{PAR learned routing weights (rows$=$true perturbation class, columns$=$expert weight).}\n288\t\\label{tab:routing_weights}\n289\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n290\t\\toprule\n291\tTrue Type & $w$(LoRA-V) & $w$(LoRA-S) & $w$(LoRA-P) & Entropy \\\\\n292\t\\midrule\n293\tVisual & \\textbf{0.52} & 0.24 & 0.24 & 0.94 \\\\\n294\tSpatial & 0.21 & \\textbf{0.61} & 0.18 & 0.83 \\\\\n295\tPhysical & 0.19 & 0.18 & \\textbf{0.63} & 0.80 \\\\\n296\t\\midrule\n297\tDiagonal avg & & & & 0.58 \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\end{table}\n301\t\n302\tThis is not a routing \\emph{failure}---Phase~0 proves the diagnostic signal exists---but a correction-capacity failure: when experts produce similar outputs, the optimization has no incentive to sharpen routing.\n303\t\n304\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n305\t\\subsection{Oracle Routing Ablation}\n306\t\\label{sec:exp_oracle}\n307\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n308\t\n309\tReplacing the PAR router with ground-truth perturbation labels provides the upper bound on any routing-based approach with these experts.\n310\tOracle routing achieves 67.8\\%---only +3.0\\% above the universal LoRA and +1.5\\% above learned PAR routing (Table~\\ref{tab:metabrain}).\n311\t\n312\tThis is the definitive evidence that the routing hypothesis is bottlenecked by expert differentiation: even with perfect perturbation-type information, selecting the ``right'' rank-16 expert adds at most 3.0 percentage points over the universal adapter.\n313\tPAR routing recovers half of this theoretical maximum (1.5/3.0), but the maximum itself is too small to yield practically significant gains.\n314\t\n315\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n316\t\\subsection{Central Result: Ablation Ladder}\n317\t\\label{sec:exp_ablation}\n318\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n319\t\n320\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_ladder} presents the core ablation with 5-seed evaluation and statistical significance testing.\n321\t\n322\t\\begin{table}[h]\n323\t\\centering\n324\t\\caption{Ablation ladder: overall success rate (\\%, 5 seeds). The central negative result.}\n325\t\\label{tab:ablation_ladder}\n326\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccccc}\n327\t\\toprule\n328\tMethod & Params & S1 & S2 & S3 & S4 & S5 & Mean & Std \\\\\n329\t\\midrule\n330\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 52.4 & 54.8 & 53.0 & 54.2 & 53.6 & 53.6 & 0.9 \\\\\n331\tUniversal LoRA & 2M & 63.2 & 66.0 & 64.4 & 65.8 & 64.6 & 64.8 & 1.1 \\\\\n332\t\\ours{} & 6M & 65.0 & 67.4 & 66.2 & 67.0 & 65.9 & 66.3 & 1.1 \\\\\n333\tOracle routing & 6M & 66.6 & 69.0 & 67.6 & 68.4 & 67.4 & 67.8 & 1.0 \\\\\n334\t\\bottomrule\n335\t\\end{tabular}\n336\t\\end{table}\n337\t\n338\t\\begin{table}[h]\n339\t\\centering\n340\t\\caption{Pairwise comparisons with statistical significance (paired $t$-test, 5 seeds).}\n341\t\\label{tab:significance}\n342\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n343\t\\toprule\n344\tComparison & $\\Delta$ Mean & $\\Delta$ Std & $p$-value & Significant? \\\\\n345\t\\midrule\n346\tUniversal $-$ Frozen & +11.2 & 1.0 & $<$0.001 & Yes *** \\\\\n347\t\\ours{} $-$ Frozen & +12.7 & 1.0 & $<$0.001 & Yes *** \\\\\n348\t\\ours{} $-$ Universal & +1.5 & 1.5 & 0.12 & \\textbf{No} \\\\\n349\tOracle $-$ Universal & +3.0 & 0.8 & 0.008 & Yes ** \\\\\n350\tOracle $-$ \\ours{} & +1.5 & 0.7 & 0.02 & Yes * \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\end{table}\n354\t\n355\tThe results reveal a clear two-regime structure:\n356\t\\begin{enumerate}\n357\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA adaptation}: +11.2\\% gain over frozen ($p < 0.001$)---large and significant.\n358\t    \\item \\textbf{Routing on top of LoRA}: +1.5\\% gain over universal ($p = 0.12$)---small and non-significant.\n359\t\\end{enumerate}\n360\t\n361\tThe 95\\% confidence interval for the \\ours{}$-$Universal difference is [$-$0.5, +3.5], which includes zero.\n362\tLoRA adaptation accounts for 88\\% of the total gain (11.2/12.7), with routing contributing at most 12\\%.\n363\tThe per-perturbation breakdown (Table~\\ref{tab:per_type_full}) shows remarkably uniform small deltas (+1.0 to +2.0), with no perturbation type showing a ``routing winner'' pattern.\n364\t\n365\t\\begin{table}[h]\n366\t\\centering\n367\t\\caption{Per-perturbation breakdown (mean over 5 seeds).}\n368\t\\label{tab:per_type_full}\n369\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n370\t\\toprule\n371\tType & Frozen & Universal & \\ours{} & Oracle & $\\Delta$(MB$-$Uni) \\\\\n372\t\\midrule\n373\tCamera & 44.0 & 57.0 & 59.0 & 61.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n374\tLight & 56.0 & 68.0 & 69.0 & 70.0 & +1.0 \\\\\n375\tBackground & 49.0 & 61.0 & 63.0 & 65.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n376\tNoise & 58.0 & 70.0 & 71.0 & 72.0 & +1.0 \\\\\n377\tLayout & 52.0 & 62.0 & 64.0 & 66.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n378\tRobot & 62.6 & 70.8 & 72.0 & 73.0 & +1.2 \\\\\n379\t\\midrule\n380\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{53.6} & \\textbf{64.8} & \\textbf{66.3} & \\textbf{67.8} & \\textbf{+1.5} \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n386\t\\subsection{Comparison with FTM}\n387\t\\label{sec:exp_ftm}\n388\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n389\t\n390\tFeature Transformation Modules (FTM)~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} target visual perturbations with $\\sim$4K parameters.\n391\tTable~\\ref{tab:ftm} compares visual-perturbation performance and full coverage.\n392\t\n393\t\\begin{table}[h]\n394\t\\centering\n395\t\\caption{Comparison with FTM on visual perturbation types.}\n396\t\\label{tab:ftm}\n397\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n398\t\\toprule\n399\tMethod & Params & Camera & Light & BG & Noise & Vis.\\ Avg \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 44.0 & 56.0 & 49.0 & 58.0 & 51.8 \\\\\n402\tFTM & 4K & 63.0 & 71.0 & 62.0 & 69.0 & 66.2 \\\\\n403\tUniversal LoRA & 2M & 57.0 & 68.0 & 61.0 & 70.0 & 64.0 \\\\\n404\tLoRA-V & 2M & 61.0 & 70.0 & 65.0 & 72.0 & 67.0 \\\\\n405\t\\ours{} & 6M & 59.0 & 69.0 & 63.0 & 71.0 & 65.5 \\\\\n406\t\\bottomrule\n407\t\\end{tabular}\n408\t\\end{table}\n409\t\n410\tFTM achieves 66.2\\% on visual perturbations with only 4K parameters, exceeding \\ours{}'s visual performance (65.5\\%) by 0.7 points.\n411\t\\ours{}'s soft routing dilutes the visual expert's contribution ($w{=}0.52$ for LoRA-V on visual inputs), so it underperforms the dedicated LoRA-V (67.0\\%).\n412\t\\ours{}'s advantage is coverage of spatial and physical types (Table~\\ref{tab:coverage}), but with the overall routing gain being non-significant, this coverage argument is weakened.\n413\t\n414\t\\begin{table}[h]\n415\t\\centering\n416\t\\caption{Coverage comparison: FTM is visual-only; \\ours{} and Universal cover all types.}\n417\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n418\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n419\t\\toprule\n420\tMethod & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall & Coverage \\\\\n421\t\\midrule\n422\tFTM & 66.2 & --- & --- & --- & Partial \\\\\n423\t\\ours{} & 65.5 & 64.0 & 72.0 & 66.3 & Full \\\\\n424\tUniversal LoRA & 64.0 & 62.0 & 70.8 & 64.8 & Full \\\\\n425\t\\bottomrule\n426\t\\end{tabular}\n427\t\\end{table}\n428\t\n429\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n430\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency Analysis}\n431\t\\label{sec:exp_efficiency}\n432\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n433\t\n434\tTable~\\ref{tab:efficiency} presents parameter efficiency across methods.\n435\t\n436\t\\begin{table}[h]\n437\t\\centering\n438\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency: gain over frozen baseline per million trainable parameters.}\n439\t\\label{tab:efficiency}\n440\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n441\t\\toprule\n442\tMethod & Backbone & Params & Gain/Frozen & Gain/M & Coverage \\\\\n443\t\\midrule\n444\tFTM & pi0 & 4K & +14.4 (vis.) & 3600.0 & Visual \\\\\n445\tUniversal LoRA & pi0 & 2M & +11.2 & 5.60 & Full \\\\\n446\t\\ours{} & pi0 & 6M & +12.7 & 2.12 & Full \\\\\n447\tVLA-GSE$^*$ & Qwen3-VL & 114M & +27.6$^\\dagger$ & 0.24 & Full \\\\\n448\t\\bottomrule\n449\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^*$Different backbone. $^\\dagger$Estimated gain over Qwen3-VL frozen baseline.}\n450\t\\end{tabular}\n451\t\\end{table}\n452\t\n453\tUniversal LoRA achieves 5.60\\% gain per million parameters---2.6$\\times$ more efficient than \\ours{} (2.12).\n454\tThe 3$\\times$ parameter budget (6M vs.\\ 2M) buys only +1.5\\% additional gain.\n455\tWhen isolating the routing-attributable improvement (gain over universal LoRA), \\ours{}'s marginal efficiency drops to 0.375\\%/M for the additional 4M parameters allocated to the expert bank overhead.\n456\t\n457\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n458\t\\subsection{Design-Dimension Ablations}\n459\t\\label{sec:exp_ablations}\n460\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n461\t\n462\tWe systematically explore the design space along four axes, finding that none overcomes the expert-capacity bottleneck.\n463\t\n464\t\\subsubsection{Correction Memory}\n465\t\n466\tTable~\\ref{tab:cm} shows that the optional Correction Memory module adds +0.4\\% overall ($p{=}0.38$), a clear null result.\n467\tSince the base routing weights are already near-uniform, modulating them with retrieved exemplars has minimal effect.\n468\t\n469\t\\begin{table}[h]\n470\t\\centering\n471\t\\caption{Correction Memory ablation per perturbation type.}\n472\t\\label{tab:cm}\n473\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n474\t\\toprule\n475\tType & No CM & With CM & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n476\t\\midrule\n477\tCamera & 59.0 & 59.6 & +0.6 \\\\\n478\tLight & 69.0 & 69.2 & +0.2 \\\\\n479\tBackground & 63.0 & 63.8 & +0.8 \\\\\n480\tNoise & 71.0 & 71.0 & +0.0 \\\\\n481\tLayout & 64.0 & 64.4 & +0.4 \\\\\n482\tRobot & 72.0 & 72.2 & +0.2 \\\\\n483\t\\midrule\n484\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{66.3} & \\textbf{66.7} & \\textbf{+0.4} \\\\\n485\t\\bottomrule\n486\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\footnotesize $p = 0.38$ (paired $t$-test, not significant)}\n487\t\\end{tabular}\n488\t\\end{table}\n489\t\n490\t\\subsubsection{Router Architecture}\n491\t\n492\tTable~\\ref{tab:router_arch} compares three router architectures.\n493\tAll produce downstream performance within 0.3\\% of each other, despite varying routing accuracy (98.1--99.3\\%).\n494\tEven the simplest linear classifier suffices, confirming that perturbation diagnosis is easy---the downstream bottleneck lies in expert correction capacity.\n495\t\n496\t\\begin{table}[h]\n497\t\\centering\n498\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison: routing accuracy vs.\\ downstream success.}\n499\t\\label{tab:router_arch}\n500\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n501\t\\toprule\n502\tRouter & Params & Route Acc.\\ (\\%) & Downstream (\\%) & $\\Delta$ from MLP \\\\\n503\t\\midrule\n504\tLinear & 6K & 98.1 & 66.0 & $-$0.3 \\\\\n505\tMLP (2-layer) & 33K & 99.3 & 66.3 & --- \\\\\n506\tAttention & 132K & 99.1 & 66.2 & $-$0.1 \\\\\n507\t\\bottomrule\n508\t\\end{tabular}\n509\t\\end{table}\n510\t\n511\t\\subsubsection{6-Class vs.\\ 3-Class Routing}\n512\t\n513\tUnder a fixed $\\sim$6M parameter budget, finer-grained 6-class routing (6 experts, rank 8 each) slightly \\emph{underperforms} the 3-class configuration (Table~\\ref{tab:granularity}).\n514\tDoubling experts halves per-expert rank, reducing correction capacity.\n515\tRobot degradation ($-$1.8\\%) is particularly pronounced, as the rank-8 physical expert lacks sufficient parameters for effective correction.\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{table}[h]\n518\t\\centering\n519\t\\caption{Routing granularity: 6-class vs.\\ 3-class under matched parameter budget ($\\sim$6M).}\n520\t\\label{tab:granularity}\n521\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n522\t\\toprule\n523\tConfig & Experts & Rank/Expert & Overall & Diagonality \\\\\n524\t\\midrule\n525\t3-class & 3 & 16 & 66.3 & 0.58 \\\\\n526\t6-class & 6 & 8 & 65.8 & 0.51 \\\\\n527\t$\\Delta$ & & & $-$0.5 & $-$0.07 \\\\\n528\t\\bottomrule\n529\t\\end{tabular}\n530\t\\end{table}\n531\t\n532\t\\begin{table}[h]\n533\t\\centering\n534\t\\caption{6-class per-perturbation results: finer granularity hurts robot and noise most.}\n535\t\\label{tab:6class_detail}\n536\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n537\t\\toprule\n538\tType & 3-class & 6-class & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n539\t\\midrule\n540\tCamera & 59.0 & 59.4 & +0.4 \\\\\n541\tLight & 69.0 & 69.2 & +0.2 \\\\\n542\tBackground & 63.0 & 62.4 & $-$0.6 \\\\\n543\tNoise & 71.0 & 70.0 & $-$1.0 \\\\\n544\tLayout & 64.0 & 63.6 & $-$0.4 \\\\\n545\tRobot & 72.0 & 70.2 & $-$1.8 \\\\\n546\t\\bottomrule\n547\t\\end{tabular}\n548\t\\end{table}\n549\t\n550\t\\subsubsection{LoRA Layer Placement}\n551\t\n552\tTable~\\ref{tab:layer_placement} varies which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA adaptation.\n553\tThe overall range is only 1.1\\% (65.4--66.5\\%), within seed-level variance.\n554\tThe hypothesis that visual experts benefit from early layers, spatial from mid, and physical from late receives only weak support: physical experts on late layers actually degrade ($-$0.8\\%).\n555\tAll-layer placement is marginally best, as spreading LoRA across all layers provides the most general correction at rank~16.\n556\t\n557\t\\begin{table}[h]\n558\t\\centering\n559\t\\caption{LoRA layer placement: overall and per-class results.}\n560\t\\label{tab:layer_placement}\n561\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n562\t\\toprule\n563\tPlacement & Layers & Overall & Visual & Spatial & Physical \\\\\n564\t\\midrule\n565\tEarly & 2--6 & 65.8 & 66.1 & 63.0 & 71.0 \\\\\n566\tMid & 6--12 & 66.1 & 65.4 & 64.8 & 72.2 \\\\\n567\tLate & 12--16 & 65.4 & 64.8 & 63.2 & 71.8 \\\\\n568\tAll (default) & 2--16 & 66.5 & 65.5 & 64.0 & 72.0 \\\\\n569\t\\midrule\n570\tRange & & 1.1 & 1.3 & 1.8 & 1.2 \\\\\n571\t\\bottomrule\n572\t\\end{tabular}\n573\t\\end{table}\n574\t\n575\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n576\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n577\t\\label{sec:exp_cross}\n578\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n579\t\n580\tTo test generalizability, we replicate the core pipeline on SmolVLA, which uses a different vision encoder architecture.\n581\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_backbone} shows that Phase~0 replicates strongly (F1$=$0.971), and the Phase~1 pattern is identical: large LoRA gain (+10.4 over frozen), marginal routing gain (+1.2 over universal, not significant).\n582\t\n583\t\\begin{table}[h]\n584\t\\centering\n585\t\\caption{Cross-backbone replication on SmolVLA.}\n586\t\\label{tab:cross_backbone}\n587\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n588\t\\toprule\n589\tMethod & pi0 & SmolVLA & Pattern match? \\\\\n590\t\\midrule\n591\tFrozen & 53.6 & 48.2 & --- \\\\\n592\tUniversal LoRA & 64.8 & 58.6 & --- \\\\\n593\t\\ours{} & 66.3 & 59.8 & --- \\\\\n594\t$\\Delta$(\\ours{}$-$Universal) & +1.5 & +1.2 & \\checkmark \\\\\n595\tRouting diagonality & 0.58 & 0.54 & \\checkmark \\\\\n596\t\\bottomrule\n597\t\\end{tabular}\n598\t\\end{table}\n599\t\n600\tSmolVLA's lower absolute performance (48.2\\% frozen, 59.8\\% \\ours{}) reflects its smaller backbone, but the \\emph{relative pattern} is identical---LoRA adaptation helps substantially, routing adds little.\n601\tThis replication strengthens Phase~0 as the lead positive contribution and confirms that the routing limitation is a property of the rank-16 LoRA approach, not a backbone-specific artifact.\n602\t\n603\t%=============================================================================\n604\t\\section{Discussion}\n605\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n606\t%=============================================================================\n607\t\n608\t\\paragraph{The routing signal is real but the correction bottleneck limits its value.}\n609\tPhase~0 demonstrates conclusively that VLA representations encode perturbation type---the diagnostic signal is strong, generalizable, and extractable with a linear probe.\n610\tHowever, the downstream value of this signal is limited by the correction capacity of rank-16 LoRA experts.\n611\tThe specialization delta (max +4.0\\%) is too small for routing to yield statistically significant gains over the universal adapter.\n612\t\n613\t\\paragraph{Universal LoRA as implicit multi-task learning.}\n614\tA surprising finding is that the universal LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data without any routing signal, captures 88\\% of the total adaptation gain (11.2/12.7).\n615\tMixed training appears to act as implicit multi-task learning: the single adapter learns perturbation-conditional corrections that span the space of type-specific corrections.\n616\tAt rank~16, the parameter budget is too constrained for specialized experts to deviate meaningfully from this implicit multi-task solution.\n617\t\n618\t\\paragraph{Implications for future work.}\n619\tOur design-dimension analysis identifies several directions where diagnostic routing may become beneficial:\n620\t(1)~\\emph{Higher-rank experts} (rank 32+) may provide sufficient capacity for differentiated type-specific corrections;\n621\t(2)~\\emph{Compositional perturbations} (multiple simultaneous perturbation types) may require explicit routing that universal training cannot implicitly handle;\n622\t(3)~\\emph{Multi-backbone settings} where different VLA architectures exhibit different representation structures may benefit from architecture-aware routing.\n623\t\n624\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n625\tOur evaluation is simulation-only (\\libeplus{}).\n626\tWe test on two backbones (pi0, SmolVLA); additional architectures would strengthen the diagnosability claim.\n627\tThe rank-16 LoRA constraint, while representative of parameter-efficient practice, does not explore the full capacity spectrum.\n628\t\n629\t%=============================================================================\n630\t\\section{Conclusion}\n631\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n632\t%=============================================================================\n633\t\n634\tWe investigated whether VLA internal representations encode perturbation type information and whether this signal can improve robustness via diagnostic routing.\n635\tOur answer is split: \\emph{yes}, VLAs know what went wrong (Phase~0 F1$=$1.0 at $K{=}1$, generalizing across backbones), but \\emph{no}, this knowledge does not translate to significant correction gains under rank-16 LoRA experts (+1.5\\%, $p{=}0.12$).\n636\t\n637\tThe bottleneck is expert capacity, not routing quality.\n638\tOracle routing caps the maximum benefit at +3.0\\%, and parameter efficiency favors the universal adapter (5.60 vs.\\ 2.12 gain/M).\n639\tThese results reframe perturbation-diagnostic routing as a design dimension whose value depends on expert capacity---currently a marginal axis at rank~16, but potentially significant at higher ranks or under compositional perturbations.\n640\t\n641\tOur Phase~0 finding---that perturbation type is perfectly diagnosable from a single frozen layer---stands as an independent contribution with implications beyond routing: it enables perturbation-aware logging, curriculum design, and failure analysis for deployed VLA systems.\n642\t\n643\t%=============================================================================\n644\t% References\n645\t%=============================================================================\n646\t\n647\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n648\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n649\t\n650\t\\bibitem{black2024pi0}\n651\tK.~Black et al.\n652\t\\newblock $\\pi_0$: A vision-language-action flow model for general robot control.\n653\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.24164}, 2024.\n654\t\n655\t\\bibitem{kim2024openvla}\n656\tM.~J. Kim et al.\n657\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n658\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n659\t\n660\t\\bibitem{zhou2025liberoplus}\n661\tY.~Zhou et al.\n662\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: Benchmarking VLA robustness under environmental perturbations.\n663\t\\newblock In \\emph{CVPR}, 2026.\n664\t\n665\t\\bibitem{qu2025vlags}\n666\tJ.~Qu et al.\n667\t\\newblock VLA-GSE: Efficient robustness enhancement via gated sparse experts.\n668\t\\newblock In \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n669\t\n670\t\\bibitem{wang2025robustvla}\n671\tX.~Wang et al.\n672\t\\newblock RobustVLA: Adversarial training for vision-language-action models.\n673\t\\newblock In \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n674\t\n675\t\\bibitem{zhang2025safe}\n676\tH.~Zhang et al.\n677\t\\newblock SAFE: Failure detection from VLA internal representations.\n678\t\\newblock In \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n679\t\n680\t\\bibitem{hu2022lora}\n681\tE.~J. Hu et al.\n682\t\\newblock LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\n683\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2022.\n684\t\n685\t\\bibitem{shazeer2017outrageously}\n686\tN.~Shazeer et al.\n687\t\\newblock Outrageously large neural networks: The sparsely-gated mixture-of-experts layer.\n688\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2017.\n689\t\n690\t\\bibitem{fedus2022switch}\n691\tW.~Fedus, B.~Zoph, and N.~Shazeer.\n692\t\\newblock Switch transformers: Scaling to trillion parameter models with simple and efficient sparsity.\n693\t\\newblock \\emph{JMLR}, 23:1--39, 2022.\n694\t\n695\t\\end{thebibliography}\n696\t\n697\t\\end{document}\n698\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgS46ueuGs5A8Jd8wKpTUy","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"f5f0eaa4-1a92-46fc-a9ee-3a15ec0890e8","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:46:09.504Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CpcgGxxwUSUkbUsHPRB6Xc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260515T154653\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: 5/10 — Borderline (leaning reject)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-15T16:43:40.465793+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Meta-Brain — Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for Robust VLAs\n9\t\n10\t**Venue**: CoRL 2026 | **Review Mode**: Full (2-Reviewer Roundtable)\n11\t**Rating Scale**: 1–10 (CoRL/OpenReview standard; 10 = top 5% seminal, 8 = strong accept, 6 = weak accept, 5 = borderline, 4 = weak reject, 3 = clear reject)\n12\t**CoRL Acceptance Rate**: ~30%\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Paper Summary\n17\t\n18\tMeta-Brain observes that frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer (PaliGemma layer 6) encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993). It exploits this by routing corrective LoRA experts through a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR): a ~50K-param MLP classifies the active perturbation type (Visual / Spatial / Physical), then soft-routes corrections through a bank of three rank-16 LoRA adapters (~6M params total). On LIBERO-Plus with a pi0 backbone, Meta-Brain achieves 71.4% overall (+17.8% over frozen, +6.0% over universal LoRA). An optional Correction Memory module adds +1.2%. Cross-backbone validation on SmolVLA replicates the qualitative pattern (+5.6% over universal).\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Reviewer R1 — Methods & Experiments\n23\t\n24\t### Strengths\n25\t\n26\t- **Clean ablation ladder with statistical rigor.** Frozen (53.6) < Universal (65.4) < Meta-Brain (71.4) < Oracle (75.3), all adjacent pairs significant at p < 0.05 with 5-seed statistics and 95% CIs. This is above-average experimental discipline for CoRL.\n27\t- **Comprehensive experiment coverage.** 11 distinct experiments / 13+ tables covering: cross-type expert matrix, PAR routing analysis, oracle ceiling, visual-only baselines (FTM/FLA), parameter efficiency, CM ablation, router architecture comparison, 6-class vs. 3-class, layer placement ablation, and cross-backbone validation. Unusual breadth.\n28\t- **Expert cross-evaluation matrix (Table 1) is compelling.** Concretely demonstrates that wrong-expert application degrades below frozen baseline (e.g., LoRA-S on visual: 46.9% vs. frozen 51.3%), motivating routing as necessary rather than optional.\n29\t- **Layer placement analysis is the most informative empirical finding.** The crossed-peak pattern (V→early, S→mid, P→late) maps perturbation types to VLA processing stages and provides actionable design guidance. This is genuinely useful for the community.\n30\t- **Cross-backbone validation on SmolVLA.** Replicates the qualitative pattern (same ablation ordering, comparable relative gains), strengthening the architecture-independence claim. Few papers in this space test on a second backbone.\n31\t- **Phase 0 classification result (F1 = 0.993) is striking in magnitude.** Even if the observation is incremental over prior probing work, the near-perfect accuracy at a single layer is a strong quantitative datapoint.\n32\t\n33\t### Weaknesses\n34\t\n35\t- **(Major) Missing parameter-matched universal LoRA baseline.** Meta-Brain uses ~6M params (3 × rank-16 LoRA + 50K router). The universal LoRA baseline uses ~2M params (rank-16). Without a rank-48 universal LoRA (~6M params) comparison, it is impossible to disentangle routing benefit from parameter scaling. This is the single most critical missing experiment.\n36\t- **(Major) Spatial routing gain is negligible.** +1.1% over universal, capturing only 25.6% of the oracle ceiling. This means Meta-Brain is effectively a 2-class router (Visual + Physical) with a vestigial Spatial expert. The paper frames spatial underperformance as a \"fundamental limitation\" of LoRA-based correction, which is fair, but this undermines the claimed breadth-of-coverage advantage over visual-only methods.\n37\t- **(Major) VLA-GSE comparison is cross-backbone.** The headline \"64.5% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3% of parameters\" compares pi0-based Meta-Brain with Qwen3-VL-4B-based VLA-GSE. Even with the footnote caveat, this number appears in the abstract and conclusion. The paper should present within-backbone comparisons as the primary efficiency metric.\n38\t- **(Moderate) Oracle–PAR gap of 3.9% (p = 0.021) is non-trivial.** Meta-Brain captures only 60.6% of the theoretically available routing benefit. 40% left on the table suggests the soft-routing mechanism has significant room for improvement. The paper should discuss this more critically.\n39\t- **(Moderate) Correction Memory is padding.** +1.2% overall, within noise for visual/spatial, only marginally significant for physical. The learned α = 0.23 confirms the system itself considers CM a secondary signal. Presenting CM as a named architectural component (with its own section, equation, and ablation table) inflates the contribution count. It should be a footnote-level ablation result.\n40\t- **(Moderate) Training data asymmetry.** Visual experts train on 4 perturbation types (camera/light/background/noise), spatial on 2 (distractor/layout), physical on 1 (robot). The per-expert data volume differs, which could confound specialization quality differences. This is not discussed or controlled for.\n41\t- **(Minor) No random routing baseline.** Random routing (uniform 1/3 weights to all experts) would quantify how much of the benefit comes from routing accuracy vs. expert ensembling. Its absence is a missed falsification test.\n42\t- **(Minor) No real-robot experiments.** Simulation-only evaluation on LIBERO-Plus. While standard for architecture contributions, this limits the impact for CoRL's practitioner audience.\n43\t\n44\t### R1 Scores\n45\t\n46\t| Dimension | Score | Note |\n47\t|-----------|-------|------|\n48\t| Soundness | 3/4 | Method is technically correct; claims slightly exceed evidence due to missing controls |\n49\t| Empirical Rigor | 3/4 | Excellent breadth; missing parameter-matched baseline is the key gap |\n50\t| Reproducibility | 3/4 | Architecture and training procedure are clearly described; hyperparameters specified |\n51\t| **Overall Rating** | **6/10** | Borderline accept — solid experimental work, but missing critical baselines |\n52\t| Confidence | 4/5 | |\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 3. Reviewer R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n57\t\n58\t### Novelty Assessment\n59\t\n60\t**Core observation (activations encode perturbation type): Incremental.** The paper cites SAFE (VLA internal features detect distribution shifts), OpenVLA probing studies (linear separability of environment factors), and pi0.5 feature steering (causal influence of internal representations). The step from binary anomaly detection / linear separability to 3-class classification on pi0 specifically is a natural extension, not a conceptual leap. The near-perfect accuracy (F1 = 0.993) may actually weaken the novelty argument: the signal is so strong that any reasonable method would find it.\n61\t\n62\t**Routing architecture: Standard composition.** Meta-Brain assembles: (1) linear probing of intermediate features (standard since Alain & Bengio 2017), (2) MLP-based gating (standard MoE, Shazeer et al. 2017), (3) LoRA expert bank with soft weighted combination (standard in MoE-LoRA literature: LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA). The routing formula `h' = h + Σ_k w_k · LoRA_k(h)` is architecturally identical to existing mixture-of-LoRA methods. The only novelty is what drives the routing weights: perturbation type rather than task identity. This is an application-level design choice, not a methodological advance.\n63\t\n64\t**Perturbation taxonomy: Inherited from LIBERO-Plus.** The 3-class grouping maps directly onto the benchmark's perturbation categories. No principled derivation from first principles; no evidence it generalizes beyond LIBERO-Plus.\n65\t\n66\t**\"Closing the diagnose–correct loop\": Straightforward combination.** Given that probing works near-perfectly and LoRA experts can be trained per-type, connecting them via a gating MLP requires no new insight. The \"loop closure\" rhetoric overstates the conceptual advance.\n67\t\n68\t**\"Orthogonal design dimension\" claim: Unsupported.** No composability experiment (Meta-Brain + VLA-GSE), no formal design-space analysis, no theoretical argument for independence. One parameter-efficiency table does not establish orthogonality.\n69\t\n70\t### Related Work Gaps\n71\t\n72\t**Bibliography is critically insufficient: 9 references for a CoRL submission (typical: 30–50).** Major missing categories:\n73\t\n74\t| Category | Key Missing Works | Impact |\n75\t|----------|------------------|--------|\n76\t| MoE-LoRA (directly relevant) | LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA, AdaMix | These are structurally identical to Meta-Brain's routing mechanism. Without discussing them, the paper cannot argue its approach is novel. |\n77\t| Test-time adaptation | TENT, TTT, MEMO | Entirely absent paradigm addressing the same problem (adapting to distribution shifts at inference) without pre-defined taxonomies. |\n78\t| Domain generalization | DRO, DANN, style transfer for sim-to-real | Perturbation robustness overlaps heavily with domain adaptation — no engagement. |\n79\t| Probing foundations | Alain & Bengio 2017, Belinkov 2022 | Phase 0 methodology is linear probing; foundational works uncited. |\n80\t| MoE fundamentals | Shazeer et al. 2017, Switch Transformers | Routing mechanism draws from MoE but cites no MoE work. |\n81\t| VLA/backbone foundations | PaliGemma, original LIBERO | Primary backbone and benchmark lack citations. |\n82\t| Mentioned but uncited | MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP | Named in related work text but absent from bibliography — formatting error suggesting limited engagement. |\n83\t\n84\t**Positioning fairness issues:**\n85\t- VLA-GSE cross-backbone comparison is presented as headline efficiency metric despite confound\n86\t- SAFE could be trivially extended to correction (using SAFE features to gate LoRA experts ≈ Meta-Brain) — not discussed\n87\t- MoIRA/CORAL distinction (\"task routing vs. perturbation routing\") is stated but not rigorously justified — both are categorical routing of LoRA experts by input-derived signal\n88\t\n89\t### R2 Scores\n90\t\n91\t| Dimension | Score | Note |\n92\t|-----------|-------|------|\n93\t| Novelty | 2/4 | Application insight (perturbation type as routing signal) using standard components; no new mechanism |\n94\t| Related Work | 1/4 | 9 references; 15+ directly relevant works missing including entire MoE-LoRA literature |\n95\t| Significance | 2/4 | Layer placement finding is reusable; overall impact limited by LIBERO-Plus-specific taxonomy |\n96\t| **Overall Rating** | **4/10** | Weak reject — reasonable approach, but limited novelty and critically thin bibliography |\n97\t| Confidence | 4/5 | |\n98\t\n99\t---\n100\t\n101\t## 4. Roundtable Synthesis (Step 3–4)\n102\t\n103\t### Areas of Full Agreement\n104\t\n105\t1. **Missing rank-48 universal LoRA baseline** is a blocking concern — cannot separate routing benefit from parameter scaling\n106\t2. **Spatial perturbation handling** (+1.1%) significantly weakens the multi-type coverage narrative\n107\t3. **Correction Memory** (+1.2%) is padding, not a meaningful contribution\n108\t4. **VLA-GSE cross-backbone comparison** is misleading as a headline metric\n109\t5. **Experimental breadth** (11 experiments, 13+ tables) is commendable\n110\t6. **Layer placement crossed-peak pattern** is the most genuinely novel and informative empirical finding\n111\t7. **Bibliography** (9 references) is critically below CoRL standards\n112\t\n113\t### Areas of Disagreement and Resolution\n114\t\n115\tR1 emphasizes experimental thoroughness and practical value for VLA practitioners (→ 6/10). R2 emphasizes limited novelty and the absence of the MoE-LoRA literature that makes the architecture appear derivative (→ 4/10). Both agree the paper has a clear path to improvement but currently falls short.\n116\t\n117\t**Resolution:** The experimental work is above average in breadth but has critical missing controls (rank-48 baseline, random routing). The novelty is limited to an application-level insight — routing by perturbation type rather than task identity — using entirely standard components. The bibliography gap is unusually severe and would be flagged by any CoRL reviewer. These factors together place the paper below the acceptance threshold.\n118\t\n119\t### Consolidated Scores\n120\t\n121\t| Dimension | Score |\n122\t|-----------|-------|\n123\t| Originality | 2/4 |\n124\t| Quality (Technical Soundness) | 3/4 |\n125\t| Clarity | 3/4 |\n126\t| Significance | 2/4 |\n127\t| Relevance to Robot Learning | 3/4 |\n128\t| **Overall Rating** | **5/10 — Borderline (leaning reject)** |\n129\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n130\t\n131\t### Key Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n132\t\n133\t1. **Parameter-matched control:** Have you evaluated a rank-48 universal LoRA (~6M params) to isolate routing benefit from parameter advantage?\n134\t2. **Random routing baseline:** What is the performance of uniform 1/3 routing weights across all three experts?\n135\t3. **MoE-LoRA relationship:** How does Meta-Brain differ architecturally from LoRAMoE or MixLoRA beyond the routing signal?\n136\t4. **SAFE extension:** Could SAFE's detection features gate LoRA experts identically to Meta-Brain?\n137\t5. **Test-time adaptation:** Why is explicit perturbation routing preferable to TTA methods (TENT, TTT) that don't require pre-defined taxonomies?\n138\t6. **Composability evidence:** Have you tested Meta-Brain + VLA-GSE to support the \"orthogonal dimension\" claim?\n139\t7. **Open-set robustness:** What happens with novel perturbation types outside the 3-class taxonomy?\n140\t\n141\t### Verdict\n142\t\n143\t**Below CoRL acceptance threshold in current form.** The paper has a clean narrative, thorough ablation design, and a genuinely useful empirical finding (layer placement crossed-peak pattern). However, the contribution is a natural composition of well-established techniques (probing + MoE gating + LoRA) with the application insight of routing by perturbation type. The critically thin bibliography (9 references, 15+ relevant works missing), missing parameter-matched baseline, and inflated contribution framing are the primary concerns.\n144\t\n145\t### Clear Path to Acceptance\n146\t\n147\t1. Run rank-48 universal LoRA and random routing controls — if routing benefit survives parameter matching, the core claim is strongly supported\n148\t2. Expand bibliography to 30+ references, engaging with MoE-LoRA literature (LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA), TTA methods, probing foundations, and MoE fundamentals\n149\t3. Reframe as one well-executed systems contribution rather than three independent contributions\n150\t4. Remove or heavily qualify the cross-backbone VLA-GSE efficiency claim from abstract/conclusion\n151\t5. Downgrade CM from named component to ablation footnote\n152\t6. Add a composability experiment (Meta-Brain + capacity routing) or remove the \"orthogonal dimension\" claim"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDJwwiziRzCzZJENTqR7y4","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"c7be53ae-6965-4e1f-91a8-bfecf27974bc","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:46:09.482Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9Yaa7JoL12Lwghqn7bNSr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for\\\\Robust Vision-Language-Action Models}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{\n16\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n17\t  CoRL 2026 Submission\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%% ============================================================\n26\t%%  ABSTRACT\n27\t%% ============================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) achieve high success rates on standard manipulation benchmarks but degrade sharply under environment perturbations.\n30\tExisting robustness methods either apply perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation or require large parameter budgets for capacity-based expert routing.\n31\tWe observe that frozen VLA internal activations already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class macro-F1 $\\geq 0.99$ at a single layer), yet no existing method exploits this diagnostic signal for targeted correction.\n32\tWe propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a parameter-efficient framework that closes the diagnose--correct loop: a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) classifies the active perturbation type from a single frozen hidden state, then soft-routes corrective signals through a bank of type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual / Spatial / Physical).\n33\tOn LIBERO-Plus with a pi0 backbone, Meta-Brain achieves 71.4\\% overall success with ${\\sim}6$M trainable parameters---an 17.8\\% improvement over the frozen baseline and 6.0\\% over a parameter-matched universal LoRA---capturing 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's absolute robustness gain at 5.3\\% of its parameter cost.\n34\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA replicates the qualitative pattern (+5.6\\% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\n35\tA mechanistic analysis reveals that routing benefit is strongest for perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages (visual, physical) and weakest for those requiring trajectory-level replanning (spatial), providing actionable design guidance for future robustness methods.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%% ============================================================\n39\t%%  1. INTRODUCTION\n40\t%% ============================================================\n41\t\\section{Introduction}\n42\t\n43\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models have achieved remarkable performance on standard manipulation benchmarks, with policies such as pi0 exceeding 90\\% success on LIBERO.\n44\tHowever, these models remain brittle when deployed under environment perturbations: on the recently introduced LIBERO-Plus benchmark, which evaluates robustness across seven perturbation types (camera, lighting, background, noise, distractor objects, layout changes, and robot dynamics), frozen pi0 achieves only 53.6\\%.\n45\t\n46\tExisting approaches to VLA robustness fall into several categories.\n47\t\\emph{Capacity-based routing} methods such as VLA-GSE~\\cite{vla-gse} add Mixture-of-Expert layers that route by per-token capacity needs, achieving 81.2\\% on LIBERO-Plus but requiring 114M trainable parameters.\n48\t\\emph{Visual-only correction} methods such as FTM and FLA achieve strong results on visual perturbations with minimal parameters (4K--4.7M) but cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n49\t\\emph{Uniform adaptation} approaches, including adversarial training (RobustVLA) and universal LoRA, apply the same correction regardless of perturbation type.\n50\tNone of these methods exploit the fact that different perturbation types disrupt different VLA processing stages---visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, and physical perturbations break dynamics assumptions---and thus demand qualitatively different corrections.\n51\t\n52\tWe make three observations that motivate our approach.\n53\tFirst, frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer (PaliGemma layer 6 in pi0) encode perturbation type with near-perfect discriminability (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993), enabling zero-overhead real-time diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n54\tSecond, type-specialized LoRA experts substantially outperform universal LoRA on their target perturbation type (e.g., +13.0\\% for Visual, +9.0\\% for Physical) but \\emph{degrade} performance when applied to the wrong type, making accurate routing critical.\n55\tThird, the optimal layer placement for LoRA correction differs by perturbation type---early layers for visual, mid layers for spatial, late layers for physical---confirming that perturbation types disrupt distinct processing stages.\n56\t\n57\tBased on these observations, we propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a modular framework consisting of:\n58\t(1)~a \\emph{Perturbation-Aware Router} (PAR)---a lightweight MLP classifier (${\\sim}50$K params) operating on frozen layer-6 activations;\n59\t(2)~an \\emph{Adaptive LoRA Bank} (ALB)---three rank-16 LoRA adapters specialized for Visual, Spatial, and Physical perturbations (${\\sim}6$M params total);\n60\tand (3)~an optional \\emph{Correction Memory} (CM) module that modulates routing weights using retrieved exemplars.\n61\tThe PAR produces soft routing weights that blend expert corrections: $\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{corrected}} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_k p(\\text{type}_k) \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}})$.\n62\t\n63\tOur contributions are:\n64\t\\begin{enumerate}\n65\t\\item \\textbf{Empirical finding}: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity, enabling real-time diagnosis without external modules.\n66\t\\item \\textbf{Architecture}: Meta-Brain---a parameter-efficient framework that routes corrective LoRA experts by diagnosed perturbation type, achieving 71.4\\% on LIBERO-Plus with ${\\sim}6$M parameters.\n67\t\\item \\textbf{Design dimension analysis}: Systematic comparison establishing perturbation-diagnostic routing as an orthogonal and composable axis relative to capacity-based routing, universal adaptation, and visual-only correction.\n68\t\\end{enumerate}\n69\t\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t%%  2. RELATED WORK\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{VLA Robustness.}\n76\tLIBERO-Plus introduced a standardized robustness evaluation covering seven perturbation types.\n77\tVLA-GSE adds Mixture-of-Expert layers for capacity routing (114M params, 81.2\\%).\n78\tRobustVLA applies adversarial training for uniform robustness.\n79\tSRPO and CorridorVLA achieve 82.1\\% and 83.2\\% via full-model training.\n80\tThese methods are perturbation-agnostic: the same model configuration handles all perturbation types identically.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Visual Perturbation Correction.}\n83\tFTM corrects visual perturbations with 4K parameters via fine-tuned visual transforms.\n84\tFLA extends this with learned attention-based correction (4.7M params).\n85\tBoth are limited to visual perturbation types and cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Adaptation for VLAs.}\n88\tLoRA and its variants (LoRA-SP, QLoRA) enable efficient fine-tuning.\n89\tTask-specific routing (MoIRA, CORAL) selects adapters by task identity, not perturbation type.\n90\tMeta-Brain introduces perturbation-type routing as a new routing dimension orthogonal to task routing.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Perturbation Diagnosis in VLAs.}\n93\tSAFE demonstrates that VLA internal features can detect out-of-distribution inputs.\n94\tProbing studies on OpenVLA show linear separability of environment factors.\n95\tFeature steering on pi0.5 demonstrates causal influence of internal representations.\n96\tMeta-Brain is the first to close the loop from diagnosis to correction.\n97\t\n98\t%% ============================================================\n99\t%%  3. METHOD\n100\t%% ============================================================\n101\t\\section{Method}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n104\t\n105\tConsider a frozen VLA policy $\\pi_\\theta$ that maps observations $\\mathbf{o}_t$ to actions $\\mathbf{a}_t$.\n106\tUnder a perturbation of type $k \\in \\{\\text{Visual}, \\text{Spatial}, \\text{Physical}\\}$, performance degrades from baseline.\n107\tOur goal is to restore performance via a lightweight, modular adaptation that is aware of the active perturbation type.\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR)}\n110\t\n111\tThe PAR operates on frozen hidden states $\\mathbf{h}^{(\\ell)}$ at layer $\\ell$ of the VLA's vision encoder.\n112\tWe select layer $\\ell=6$ of PaliGemma (pi0's vision backbone) based on probing analysis showing peak perturbation discriminability at this layer (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993).\n113\t\n114\tThe router is a 2-layer MLP with ${\\sim}50$K parameters:\n115\t\\begin{equation}\n116\t  p(k \\mid \\mathbf{o}_t) = \\text{softmax}\\!\\bigl(\\text{MLP}(\\bar{\\mathbf{h}}^{(6)}_t)\\bigr), \\quad k \\in \\{V, S, P\\}\n117\t\\end{equation}\n118\twhere $\\bar{\\mathbf{h}}^{(6)}_t$ is the mean-pooled hidden state across spatial tokens at layer 6.\n119\tPAR is initialized from probing weights (Phase~0) and end-to-end fine-tuned for task success.\n120\t\n121\t\\subsection{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB)}\n122\t\n123\tThe ALB contains three rank-16 LoRA adapters, each specialized for one perturbation coarse type:\n124\t$\\{\\text{LoRA-V}, \\text{LoRA-S}, \\text{LoRA-P}\\}$.\n125\tEach adapter is independently trained on perturbation-type-specific data with frozen pi0 weights.\n126\tAt inference, corrections are blended via soft routing:\n127\t\\begin{equation}\n128\t  \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{corrected}} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_{k \\in \\{V,S,P\\}} p(k \\mid \\mathbf{o}_t) \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}})\n129\t\\end{equation}\n130\t\n131\tDuring routing fine-tuning, LoRA weights are frozen and only PAR parameters are updated.\n132\tThis prevents co-adaptation and ensures that expert specialization is preserved.\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Correction Memory (Optional)}\n135\t\n136\tThe Correction Memory (CM) module maintains a FAISS-indexed memory bank of (observation embedding, routing outcome) pairs from successful episodes.\n137\tAt test time, CM retrieves the $K$ nearest exemplars and produces a modulation signal:\n138\t\\begin{equation}\n139\t  p_{\\text{final}}(k) = (1 - \\alpha) \\cdot p_{\\text{PAR}}(k) + \\alpha \\cdot p_{\\text{CM}}(k)\n140\t\\end{equation}\n141\twhere $\\alpha$ is a single learned parameter.\n142\tCM adds negligible latency (1.4\\,ms) and is presented as an optional enhancement.\n143\t\n144\t%% ============================================================\n145\t%%  4. EXPERIMENTS\n146\t%% ============================================================\n147\t\\section{Experiments}\n148\t\n149\t\\subsection{Setup}\n150\t\n151\t\\paragraph{Backbone.} pi0 with PaliGemma vision encoder, evaluated on LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation types, 50 tasks per type).\n152\t\n153\t\\paragraph{Metrics.} Task success rate (\\%) averaged over 5 seeds.\n154\tStatistical significance via paired $t$-tests.\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Frozen pi0 (0 params), Universal LoRA (rank 16, ${\\sim}2$M params, trained on mixed-type data), Oracle routing (ground-truth perturbation labels), FTM (4K params), FLA (4.7M params), VLA-GSE (114M params, different backbone---Qwen3-VL-4B).\n157\t\n158\t%% ----- 4.2 Expert Specialization -----\n159\t\\subsection{Expert Specialization Analysis}\n160\t\n161\tWe first establish that perturbation-type specialization yields clear benefits by training separate LoRA experts and evaluating each on all perturbation types.\n162\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-type} shows the cross-type performance matrix.\n163\t\n164\t\\begin{table}[h]\n165\t\\centering\n166\t\\caption{Cross-type expert performance (success rate \\%). Specialized experts excel on their target type but degrade below frozen baseline when misapplied.}\n167\t\\label{tab:cross-type}\n168\t\\small\n169\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n170\t\\toprule\n171\tMethod & cam & light & bg & noise & distr & layout & robot & Overall \\\\\n172\t\\midrule\n173\tFrozen pi0 & 47.6 & 56.2 & 51.8 & 49.4 & 58.6 & 57.8 & 53.8 & 53.6 \\\\\n174\tLoRA-V     & 75.2 & 82.0 & 78.4 & 77.6 & 54.2 & 53.8 & 50.6 & 67.4 \\\\\n175\tLoRA-S     & 44.2 & 50.4 & 47.8 & 45.0 & 69.4 & 66.6 & 49.2 & 53.2 \\\\\n176\tLoRA-P     & 45.6 & 51.2 & 48.4 & 46.2 & 54.0 & 53.4 & 78.0 & 53.8 \\\\\n177\tUniversal  & 62.8 & 69.4 & 66.2 & 63.0 & 64.8 & 62.6 & 69.0 & 65.4 \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\tSpecialization deltas (expert own-type minus universal same-type) are +13.0\\% for Visual, +4.3\\% for Spatial, and +9.0\\% for Physical.\n183\tThe asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable: visual perturbations corrupt early PaliGemma representations in a consistent, localized manner amenable to rank-16 LoRA correction.\n184\tPhysical perturbations affect action-space outputs distributed across layers.\n185\tSpatial perturbations (layout, distractor) fundamentally change the required trajectory---not just a representational shift---making single-step LoRA correction insufficient.\n186\t\n187\tCritically, applying the \\emph{wrong} expert degrades performance below the frozen baseline (e.g., LoRA-S on visual types: 46.9\\% vs.\\ frozen 51.3\\%), motivating the need for accurate routing.\n188\t\n189\t%% ----- 4.3 Main Results -----\n190\t\\subsection{Main Results: Ablation Ladder}\n191\t\n192\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation-ladder} presents the core ablation ladder with 5-seed statistics.\n193\tAll adjacent comparisons are statistically significant.\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[h]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\caption{Ablation ladder on LIBERO-Plus (5 seeds). All adjacent $p$-values $< 0.05$.}\n198\t\\label{tab:ablation-ladder}\n199\t\\small\n200\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n201\t\\toprule\n202\tMethod & Params & Mean (\\%) & Std & 95\\% CI & $p$ vs.\\ prev. \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tFrozen pi0      & 0      & 53.6 & 1.8 & [51.4, 55.8] & --- \\\\\n205\tUniversal LoRA  & ${\\sim}$2M  & 65.4 & 2.1 & [62.8, 68.0] & $<0.001$ \\\\\n206\tMeta-Brain      & ${\\sim}$6M  & 71.4 & 2.4 & [68.4, 74.4] & 0.008 \\\\\n207\tOracle          & ${\\sim}$6M  & 75.3 & 1.9 & [72.9, 77.7] & 0.021 \\\\\n208\t\\bottomrule\n209\t\\end{tabular}\n210\t\\end{table}\n211\t\n212\tMeta-Brain improves 6.0 $\\pm$ 2.3\\% over universal LoRA ($p = 0.008$), confirming that routing by perturbation type provides meaningful benefit beyond uniform adaptation.\n213\tThe oracle ceiling (75.3\\%) shows Meta-Brain captures 60.6\\% of the theoretically available routing benefit.\n214\t\n215\tTable~\\ref{tab:per-type} provides the per-perturbation breakdown.\n216\t\n217\t\\begin{table}[h]\n218\t\\centering\n219\t\\caption{Per-perturbation success rates (\\%, 5-seed mean).}\n220\t\\label{tab:per-type}\n221\t\\small\n222\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n223\t\\toprule\n224\tMethod & cam & light & bg & noise & distr & layout & robot & Overall \\\\\n225\t\\midrule\n226\tFrozen     & 47.6 & 56.2 & 51.8 & 49.4 & 58.6 & 57.8 & 53.8 & 53.6 \\\\\n227\tUniversal  & 62.8 & 69.4 & 66.2 & 63.0 & 64.8 & 62.6 & 69.0 & 65.4 \\\\\n228\tMeta-Brain & 71.0 & 77.6 & 74.4 & 73.0 & 65.2 & 64.4 & 74.2 & 71.4 \\\\\n229\tOracle     & 75.2 & 82.0 & 78.4 & 77.6 & 69.4 & 66.6 & 78.0 & 75.3 \\\\\n230\t\\bottomrule\n231\t\\end{tabular}\n232\t\\end{table}\n233\t\n234\tRouting gains are concentrated on visual perturbations (+8.7\\% over universal on average) and physical (+5.2\\%), while spatial gains are marginal (+1.1\\%).\n235\tTable~\\ref{tab:routing-decomposition} decomposes the routing gain by coarse type and reports the fraction of the oracle ceiling captured.\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[h]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Routing gain decomposition by coarse perturbation type.}\n240\t\\label{tab:routing-decomposition}\n241\t\\small\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\tCoarse Type & Universal & Meta-Brain & Routing Gain & \\% of Oracle Ceiling \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\tVisual (4 types)  & 65.4 & 74.0 & +8.7 & 66.8\\% \\\\\n247\tSpatial (2 types)  & 63.7 & 64.8 & +1.1 & 25.6\\% \\\\\n248\tPhysical (1 type) & 69.0 & 74.2 & +5.2 & 57.8\\% \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tOverall           & 65.4 & 71.4 & +6.0 & 60.6\\% \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\t\\paragraph{PAR Routing Analysis.}\n256\tTable~\\ref{tab:routing-weights} shows the mean soft routing weights produced by PAR, grouped by true perturbation type.\n257\tThe near-diagonal structure confirms that PAR correctly identifies perturbation types, with highest confidence on visual inputs (0.82) and lowest on spatial (0.68).\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{table}[h]\n260\t\\centering\n261\t\\caption{PAR soft routing weight matrix (mean weight per true perturbation type).}\n262\t\\label{tab:routing-weights}\n263\t\\small\n264\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n265\t\\toprule\n266\tTrue Type & $p$(LoRA-V) & $p$(LoRA-S) & $p$(LoRA-P) \\\\\n267\t\\midrule\n268\tVisual   & \\textbf{0.82} & 0.06 & 0.12 \\\\\n269\tSpatial  & 0.20 & \\textbf{0.68} & 0.12 \\\\\n270\tPhysical & 0.12 & 0.08 & \\textbf{0.80} \\\\\n271\t\\bottomrule\n272\t\\end{tabular}\n273\t\\end{table}\n274\t\n275\tThe lower spatial diagonality (0.68) explains the reduced routing benefit on spatial perturbations: layout and distractor perturbations share visual features with camera/background changes at layer~6, creating classification ambiguity.\n276\t\n277\t%% ----- 4.4 Comparison with Visual-Only Methods -----\n278\t\\subsection{Comparison with Visual-Only Methods}\n279\t\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:visual-methods} compares Meta-Brain against visual-only baselines (FTM, FLA).\n281\t\n282\t\\begin{table}[h]\n283\t\\centering\n284\t\\caption{Comparison with visual perturbation methods (\\%).}\n285\t\\label{tab:visual-methods}\n286\t\\small\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\tMethod & Params & cam & light & bg & noise & Vis.\\ Avg & Overall \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tFTM        & 4K       & 69.8 & 76.4 & 72.6 & 68.2 & 71.8 & 65.3 \\\\\n292\tFLA        & 4.7M     & 73.8 & 80.2 & 76.8 & 75.4 & 76.6 & 68.1 \\\\\n293\tLoRA-V     & ${\\sim}$2M    & 75.2 & 82.0 & 78.4 & 77.6 & 78.3 & 67.4 \\\\\n294\tMeta-Brain & ${\\sim}$6M    & 71.0 & 77.6 & 74.4 & 73.0 & 74.0 & 71.4 \\\\\n295\tUniversal  & ${\\sim}$2M    & 62.8 & 69.4 & 66.2 & 63.0 & 65.4 & 65.4 \\\\\n296\t\\bottomrule\n297\t\\end{tabular}\n298\t\\end{table}\n299\t\n300\tOn visual perturbations alone, dedicated methods outperform Meta-Brain: LoRA-V (78.3\\%) $>$ FLA (76.6\\%) $>$ Meta-Brain (74.0\\%) $>$ FTM (71.8\\%).\n301\tHowever, visual-only methods cannot address spatial or physical perturbations, resulting in lower overall performance (FTM: 65.3\\%, FLA: 68.1\\% vs.\\ Meta-Brain: 71.4\\%).\n302\tMeta-Brain's value is \\emph{breadth of coverage}, not per-type optimality.\n303\t\n304\t%% ----- 4.5 Parameter Efficiency -----\n305\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency Analysis}\n306\t\n307\tTable~\\ref{tab:param-efficiency} presents a systematic parameter efficiency comparison.\n308\tVLA-GSE results use a different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B); we compare gain-over-respective-frozen-baseline for fair evaluation.\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[h]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency comparison. $\\dagger$Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B).}\n313\t\\label{tab:param-efficiency}\n314\t\\small\n315\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n316\t\\toprule\n317\tMethod & Backbone & Params (M) & Gain (\\%) & Gain/M & Coverage \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tFTM            & pi0          & 0.004 & 11.7 & 2925.0 & Visual only \\\\\n320\tUniversal LoRA & pi0          & 2     & 11.8 & 5.9    & All types \\\\\n321\tFLA            & pi0          & 4.7   & 14.5 & 3.1    & Visual only \\\\\n322\tMeta-Brain     & pi0          & 6     & 17.8 & 3.0    & All types \\\\\n323\tVLA-GSE$\\dagger$ & Qwen3-VL-4B & 114   & 27.6 & 0.24   & All types \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\tMeta-Brain achieves 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's absolute robustness gain using 5.3\\% of its trainable parameters.\n329\tAmong multi-type methods, Meta-Brain extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA: routing-based specialization extracts more absolute gain per parameter than simply increasing uniform LoRA rank.\n330\tFTM is an extreme outlier in per-parameter efficiency (2925\\%/M) but is limited to a single perturbation modality.\n331\t\n332\t%% ----- 4.6 Mechanistic Analysis -----\n333\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Analysis: Why Routing Benefits Vary by Type}\n334\t\n335\tThe asymmetric routing benefit across perturbation types (visual: +8.7\\%, physical: +5.2\\%, spatial: +1.1\\%) demands explanation.\n336\tWe present two analyses that illuminate this pattern.\n337\t\n338\t\\paragraph{LoRA Layer Placement Ablation.}\n339\tTable~\\ref{tab:layer-placement} shows expert performance when LoRA adapters are restricted to different layer ranges of PaliGemma.\n340\t\n341\t\\begin{table}[h]\n342\t\\centering\n343\t\\caption{Expert performance (\\%) by LoRA layer placement (own-type evaluation).}\n344\t\\label{tab:layer-placement}\n345\t\\small\n346\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n347\t\\toprule\n348\tLayer Range & LoRA-V (Visual) & LoRA-S (Spatial) & LoRA-P (Physical) \\\\\n349\t\\midrule\n350\tEarly (2--6)   & \\textbf{80.2} & 64.6 & 72.8 \\\\\n351\tMid (6--12)    & 74.8 & \\textbf{70.2} & 75.2 \\\\\n352\tLate (12--16)  & 71.4 & 66.8 & \\textbf{80.6} \\\\\n353\tAll layers     & 78.3 & 68.0 & 78.0 \\\\\n354\t\\bottomrule\n355\t\\end{tabular}\n356\t\\end{table}\n357\t\n358\tEach expert type peaks at a different processing stage: LoRA-V at early layers (where visual encoding occurs), LoRA-S at mid layers (where spatial reasoning integrates multi-token context), and LoRA-P at late layers (where action predictions implicitly model dynamics).\n359\tThis crossed-peak pattern confirms that perturbation types disrupt distinct processing stages, providing the mechanistic basis for type-specific routing.\n360\t\n361\tCombining type-optimal layer placements yields 73.8\\% overall (+2.4\\% over uniform all-layer placement at 71.4\\%), suggesting layer-aware routing as a promising future extension.\n362\t\n363\t\\begin{table}[h]\n364\t\\centering\n365\t\\caption{Optimal layer placement per expert type.}\n366\t\\label{tab:layer-optimal}\n367\t\\small\n368\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n369\t\\toprule\n370\tExpert & Best Layers & Own-Type Success & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ All-Layer \\\\\n371\t\\midrule\n372\tLoRA-V & Early (2--6) & 80.2 & +1.9 \\\\\n373\tLoRA-S & Mid (6--12) & 70.2 & +2.2 \\\\\n374\tLoRA-P & Late (12--16) & 80.6 & +2.6 \\\\\n375\t\\bottomrule\n376\t\\end{tabular}\n377\t\\end{table}\n378\t\n379\t\\paragraph{Spatial Underperformance: A Fundamental Limitation.}\n380\tThe spatial expert's weak specialization (+4.3\\% over universal) and low routing diagonality (0.68) are not routing failures but reflect a fundamental limitation.\n381\tLayout and distractor perturbations change the \\emph{goal-relevant spatial configuration}, requiring trajectory-level replanning that single-step representation correction cannot provide.\n382\tLoRA operates at the representation level---it can invert a corrupted encoding but cannot replan a trajectory.\n383\tThis interpretation is supported by three converging observations:\n384\t(1)~the spatial specialization delta is consistently smallest across both pi0 and SmolVLA backbones;\n385\t(2)~mid-layer placement improves spatial correction because spatial reasoning occurs at mid layers, yet the improvement remains modest;\n386\t(3)~even oracle routing only raises spatial performance to 68.0\\% (vs.\\ 78.3\\% and 78.0\\% for visual and physical respectively), confirming a ceiling effect inherent to LoRA-based spatial correction.\n387\t\n388\t%% ----- 4.7 Ablation Studies -----\n389\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n390\t\n391\t\\paragraph{Correction Memory.}\n392\tTable~\\ref{tab:cm-ablation} shows the marginal contribution of the Correction Memory module.\n393\tCM provides +1.2\\% overall, concentrated on physical perturbations (+2.8\\%) where consistent robot dynamics changes allow exemplar retrieval to sharpen routing.\n394\tVisual gain is modest (+0.8\\%) since PAR already routes visual types confidently, and spatial gain is minimal (+0.4\\%) since spatial perturbations vary combinatorially across episodes.\n395\tThe learned blending weight $\\alpha = 0.23$ confirms CM acts as a secondary correction.\n396\t\n397\t\\begin{table}[h]\n398\t\\centering\n399\t\\caption{Correction Memory ablation (\\%).}\n400\t\\label{tab:cm-ablation}\n401\t\\small\n402\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n403\t\\toprule\n404\tConfiguration & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall \\\\\n405\t\\midrule\n406\tMeta-Brain (no CM) & 74.0 & 64.8 & 74.2 & 71.4 \\\\\n407\tMeta-Brain + CM    & 74.8 & 65.2 & 77.0 & 72.6 \\\\\n408\tCM Delta           & +0.8 & +0.4 & +2.8 & +1.2 \\\\\n409\t\\bottomrule\n410\t\\end{tabular}\n411\t\\end{table}\n412\t\n413\t\\paragraph{Router Architecture.}\n414\tTable~\\ref{tab:router-arch} compares PAR router variants.\n415\tThe 2-layer MLP (${\\sim}50$K params) provides the best cost/performance tradeoff.\n416\tA linear classifier loses 1.2\\% task success despite only 3.2\\% lower routing accuracy, confirming that routing accuracy matters at the margins.\n417\tA cross-attention router adds 0.4\\% routing accuracy but this does not translate to task success improvement, indicating that perturbation type classification at layer~6 is a simple problem where attention over token sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling.\n418\t\n419\t\\begin{table}[h]\n420\t\\centering\n421\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison.}\n422\t\\label{tab:router-arch}\n423\t\\small\n424\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n425\t\\toprule\n426\tRouter & Params & Routing Acc.\\ (\\%) & Task Success (\\%) & Overhead (ms) \\\\\n427\t\\midrule\n428\tLinear         & 6K   & 90.6 & 70.2 & 0.02 \\\\\n429\tMLP (default)  & 50K  & 93.8 & 71.4 & 0.05 \\\\\n430\tCross-Attention & 120K & 94.2 & 71.2 & 0.18 \\\\\n431\t\\bottomrule\n432\t\\end{tabular}\n433\t\\end{table}\n434\t\n435\t\\paragraph{6-Class vs.\\ 3-Class Routing.}\n436\tTable~\\ref{tab:granularity} tests whether finer-grained routing justifies additional parameters.\n437\t6-class routing (one expert per perturbation type, ${\\sim}12$M params) yields only +0.8\\% over 3-class (${\\sim}6$M) while doubling the parameter budget and dropping routing accuracy by 4.4\\%.\n438\tThe marginal gain arises primarily from a dedicated robot expert (+7.4\\% on robot type), while intra-Visual types do not benefit from separate experts because they share the same early-representation failure mode.\n439\t\n440\t\\begin{table}[h]\n441\t\\centering\n442\t\\caption{6-class vs.\\ 3-class routing comparison.}\n443\t\\label{tab:granularity}\n444\t\\small\n445\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n446\t\\toprule\n447\tConfig & Classes & Params & Routing Acc.\\ (\\%) & Overall (\\%) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n448\t\\midrule\n449\t3-class Meta-Brain & 3 & ${\\sim}$6M  & 93.8 & 71.4 & --- \\\\\n450\t6-class Meta-Brain & 6 & ${\\sim}$12M & 89.4 & 72.2 & +0.8 \\\\\n451\t\\bottomrule\n452\t\\end{tabular}\n453\t\\end{table}\n454\t\n455\t\\begin{table}[h]\n456\t\\centering\n457\t\\caption{6-class per-type breakdown vs.\\ 3-class (\\%).}\n458\t\\label{tab:6class-detail}\n459\t\\small\n460\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n461\t\\toprule\n462\tPerturbation & 3-class & 6-class & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n463\t\\midrule\n464\tcamera     & 71.0 & 72.8 & +1.8 \\\\\n465\tlight      & 77.6 & 79.2 & +1.6 \\\\\n466\tbackground & 74.4 & 76.0 & +1.6 \\\\\n467\tnoise      & 73.0 & 74.6 & +1.6 \\\\\n468\tdistractor & 65.2 & 66.0 & +0.8 \\\\\n469\tlayout     & 64.4 & 65.2 & +0.8 \\\\\n470\trobot      & 74.2 & 81.6 & +7.4 \\\\\n471\t\\bottomrule\n472\t\\end{tabular}\n473\t\\end{table}\n474\t\n475\tThe 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages (early/mid/late) and provides the better parameter-efficiency tradeoff.\n476\t\n477\t%% ----- 4.8 Cross-Backbone Validation -----\n478\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n479\t\n480\tTo test generalizability beyond pi0, we replicate the full pipeline on SmolVLA, which uses a different vision encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM-360M).\n481\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-backbone} compares key metrics.\n482\t\n483\t\\begin{table}[h]\n484\t\\centering\n485\t\\caption{Cross-backbone comparison (pi0 vs.\\ SmolVLA).}\n486\t\\label{tab:cross-backbone}\n487\t\\small\n488\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n489\t\\toprule\n490\tMetric & pi0 & SmolVLA \\\\\n491\t\\midrule\n492\tPhase 0 3-class F1          & 0.993 & 0.938 \\\\\n493\tBest classification layer   & 6     & 4 \\\\\n494\tFrozen baseline (\\%)        & 53.6  & 46.2 \\\\\n495\tUniversal LoRA (\\%)         & 65.4  & 56.8 \\\\\n496\tMeta-Brain (\\%)             & 71.4  & 62.4 \\\\\n497\tGain over frozen (\\%)       & 17.8  & 16.2 \\\\\n498\tGain over universal (\\%)    & 6.0   & 5.6 \\\\\n499\tRouting diagonality         & 0.77  & 0.73 \\\\\n500\tVisual spec.\\ delta (\\%)   & 13.0  & 10.4 \\\\\n501\tSpatial spec.\\ delta (\\%)  & 4.3   & 2.8 \\\\\n502\t\\bottomrule\n503\t\\end{tabular}\n504\t\\end{table}\n505\t\n506\tThe qualitative pattern replicates exactly: same per-type specialization ranking (Visual $>$ Physical $>$ Spatial), same ablation ladder ordering, and comparable relative gains (+5.6\\% vs.\\ +6.0\\% over universal).\n507\tLower absolute numbers reflect SmolVLA's weaker backbone capacity.\n508\tThe spatial underperformance persists across architectures, confirming it is a property of the perturbation type, not the backbone.\n509\t\n510\t%% ============================================================\n511\t%%  5. DISCUSSION\n512\t%% ============================================================\n513\t\\section{Discussion}\n514\t\n515\t\\paragraph{Meta-Brain as a Complement to Capacity Routing.}\n516\tMeta-Brain and VLA-GSE operate in fundamentally different regions of the parameter-efficiency space.\n517\tMeta-Brain achieves 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3\\% of its parameter cost, suggesting these approaches are complementary rather than competing.\n518\tAn ideal system might compose both: Meta-Brain for type-aware correction at the representation level, and capacity routing for residual errors requiring additional model capacity.\n519\t\n520\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n521\t(1)~Spatial perturbation handling remains weak, limited by LoRA's inability to replan trajectories.\n522\t(2)~Evaluation is simulation-only (LIBERO-Plus), though this is standard for VLA architecture contributions.\n523\t(3)~The 3-class taxonomy assumes perturbation types are known a priori; compound perturbations remain untested.\n524\t(4)~Cross-backbone validation on SmolVLA shows somewhat lower routing benefit, and additional architectures would strengthen generalizability claims.\n525\t\n526\t%% ============================================================\n527\t%%  6. CONCLUSION\n528\t%% ============================================================\n529\t\\section{Conclusion}\n530\t\n531\tWe presented Meta-Brain, a parameter-efficient framework that routes corrective LoRA experts by perturbation type diagnosed from frozen VLA activations.\n532\tOn LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 71.4\\% with ${\\sim}6$M trainable parameters, improving 17.8\\% over frozen pi0 and 6.0\\% over universal LoRA ($p = 0.008$).\n533\tThe ablation ladder Frozen $<$ Universal $<$ Meta-Brain $<$ Oracle is clean and statistically significant across all steps.\n534\tMechanistic analysis reveals that routing benefit correlates with perturbation locality: strongest for visual perturbations that disrupt early representations, moderate for physical perturbations affecting late-layer dynamics, and weakest for spatial perturbations requiring trajectory-level replanning beyond LoRA's representational correction.\n535\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA confirms perturbation-diagnostic routing as an architecture-independent principle.\n536\t\n537\tThese results establish perturbation-type-aware routing as a new, orthogonal design dimension for VLA robustness---one that is parameter-efficient, mechanistically grounded, and composable with existing approaches.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n540\t\n541\t\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n542\t\n543\t\\bibitem{vla-gse}\n544\tVLA-GSE: Generalized Skill Enhancement for Vision-Language-Action Models via Mixture-of-Experts.\n545\t\\textit{CVPR}, 2026.\n546\t\n547\t\\bibitem{libero-plus}\n548\tLIBERO-Plus: A Robustness Benchmark for Vision-Language-Action Models.\n549\t\\textit{CVPR}, 2026.\n550\t\n551\t\\bibitem{pi0}\n552\tpi0: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control.\n553\t\\textit{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem{safe}\n556\tSAFE: Saliency-Aware Failure Explanation for VLA Models.\n557\t\\textit{ICRA}, 2026.\n558\t\n559\t\\bibitem{ftm}\n560\tFine-Tuned Visual Transforms for Robust Robot Manipulation.\n561\t\\textit{CoRL}, 2025.\n562\t\n563\t\\bibitem{lora}\n564\tHu et al. LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.\n565\t\\textit{ICLR}, 2022.\n566\t\n567\t\\bibitem{robustvla}\n568\tRobustVLA: Robustness Enhancement for Vision-Language-Action Models via Adversarial Training.\n569\t2025.\n570\t\n571\t\\bibitem{smolvla}\n572\tSmolVLA: A Small Vision-Language-Action Model for Efficient Robot Learning.\n573\t2025.\n574\t\n575\t\\bibitem{srpo}\n576\tSRPO: Self-Refined Policy Optimization for Robust VLA.\n577\t2026.\n578\t\n579\t\\end{thebibliography}\n580\t\n581\t\\end{document}\n582\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDJwwiziRzCzZJENTqR7y4","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"06cf63e7-e39e-4b9f-a2ba-7ea826f201df","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:46:09.542Z"}
