{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UqWfFmFCq42JgkhXDQbgZH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D1JCYEnNWrXkM476dWMu2B","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/worst/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28210,"cache_read_input_tokens":27406,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":28210,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDJwwiziRzCzZJENTqR7y4","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"06303094-d5b9-43e1-a2f1-4473199113f9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D1JCYEnNWrXkM476dWMu2B","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n4\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n12\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{Meta-Brain}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\libeplus}{LIBERO-Plus}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong?\\\\Probing Perturbation Representations and\\\\the Limits of Diagnostic Routing}\n18\t\n19\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n20\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n21\t\n22\t\\date{CoRL 2026 Submission}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t%=============================================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t%=============================================================================\n30\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) suffer severe performance degradation under environmental perturbations, yet it remains unknown whether their internal representations encode information about \\emph{what type} of perturbation occurred.\n31\tWe present two findings.\n32\t\\textbf{First}, frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy: a linear probe on PaliGemma layer~6 of pi0 achieves macro-F1$=$1.0 at $K{=}1$ (one observation step), and this result generalizes to SmolVLA (F1$=$0.971).\n33\tThis establishes \\emph{perturbation diagnosability} as a general property of VLA representations.\n34\t\\textbf{Second}, we test whether this diagnostic signal can be exploited for targeted correction via \\emph{perturbation-diagnostic routing}: a lightweight router directs inference through type-specialized LoRA experts instead of a single universal adapter.\n35\tOn \\libeplus{}, the routed system (\\ours{}, 6M params) achieves 66.3\\% overall success, a +12.7\\% gain over the frozen baseline but only +1.5\\% over a universal LoRA (64.8\\%), a difference that is \\emph{not statistically significant} ($p{=}0.12$, 5-seed paired $t$-test).\n36\tOracle routing with ground-truth labels yields 67.8\\%, capping the routing benefit at 3.0\\%.\n37\tWe trace the bottleneck to expert capacity: at rank~16, specialized LoRA adapters converge to similar corrections regardless of perturbation type, leaving little margin for routing to exploit.\n38\tOur results map the design landscape for perturbation-aware VLA adaptation and identify expert capacity---not routing quality---as the binding constraint.\n39\t\\end{abstract}\n40\t\n41\t%=============================================================================\n42\t\\section{Introduction}\n43\t\\label{sec:intro}\n44\t%=============================================================================\n45\t\n46\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) such as pi0~\\cite{black2024pi0} and OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} achieve high success rates on standard manipulation benchmarks, but their performance drops sharply under environmental perturbations.\n47\tOn \\libeplus{}~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus}, a robustness benchmark spanning six perturbation categories, a frozen pi0 backbone achieves only 53.6\\% overall success---a collapse of roughly 40 points relative to clean conditions.\n48\t\n49\tExisting robustness methods address this gap through diverse strategies: feature transformation modules (FTM)~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} target visual perturbations with remarkable parameter efficiency ($\\sim$4K params); VLA-GSE~\\cite{qu2025vlags} routes tokens through capacity-based Mixture-of-Experts with 114M trainable parameters; RobustVLA~\\cite{wang2025robustvla} applies perturbation-agnostic adversarial training.\n50\tNone of these methods ask a fundamental representational question: \\emph{do VLA internal activations encode what type of perturbation is present?}\n51\t\n52\tWe answer this question affirmatively.\n53\tBy training linear probes on frozen intermediate-layer activations of pi0's PaliGemma vision encoder, we find that perturbation type (Visual / Spatial / Physical) is diagnosable with macro-F1$\\geq$0.99 from a single layer (layer~6) at the very first observation step ($K{=}1$).\n54\tThis finding replicates on SmolVLA (F1$=$0.971 at layer~4), suggesting that \\emph{perturbation diagnosability} is a general property of VLA representations rather than a backbone-specific artifact.\n55\t\n56\tGiven that the diagnostic signal is strong, a natural hypothesis follows: routing corrective adaptation through type-specialized experts should outperform perturbation-agnostic universal adaptation.\n57\tWe test this hypothesis with \\ours{}, a modular architecture that combines a Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) with a bank of type-specialized LoRA adapters.\n58\tThe result is a \\emph{systematic negative finding}: while \\ours{} improves over the frozen baseline by +12.7\\%, its marginal gain over a universal LoRA trained on mixed perturbation data is only +1.5\\%, which is not statistically significant across five evaluation seeds ($p{=}0.12$).\n59\tAn oracle ablation with ground-truth perturbation labels caps the maximum routing benefit at +3.0\\%.\n60\t\n61\tWe trace the bottleneck to \\emph{expert capacity}: at rank~16, LoRA adapters lack sufficient parameters to learn meaningfully different type-specific corrections.\n62\tThe maximum specialization advantage of any expert over the universal adapter is only +4.0 percentage points.\n63\tA universal LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data, implicitly learns perturbation-conditional corrections that capture most of the specialization benefit.\n64\t\n65\tOur contributions are:\n66\t\\begin{enumerate}\n67\t    \\item \\textbf{Perturbation diagnosability.} We show that frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity across two architectures (pi0 and SmolVLA), enabling zero-overhead real-time diagnosis.\n68\t    \\item \\textbf{Informative negative result.} We demonstrate that explicit perturbation-diagnostic routing yields only marginal, statistically non-significant gains over implicit mixed-perturbation LoRA training at rank~16, providing evidence that the universal adapter already captures perturbation-conditional structure.\n69\t    \\item \\textbf{Design-dimension analysis.} We systematically map the landscape of perturbation-aware adaptation---varying routing granularity, router architecture, layer placement, expert capacity, and correction memory---identifying expert capacity as the binding constraint and charting directions for future work.\n70\t\\end{enumerate}\n71\t\n72\t%=============================================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\\label{sec:related}\n75\t%=============================================================================\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{VLA robustness.}\n78\t\\libeplus{}~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} introduced a standardized perturbation benchmark with six categories and proposed Feature Transformation Modules (FTM) for visual robustness.\n79\tVLA-GSE~\\cite{qu2025vlags} applies Mixture-of-Experts routing based on per-token capacity needs, achieving 81.2\\% with 114M trainable parameters on a Qwen3-VL backbone.\n80\tRobustVLA~\\cite{wang2025robustvla} improves robustness via adversarial training.\n81\tSAFE~\\cite{zhang2025safe} detects perturbations from internal representations but does not close the loop to type-specific correction.\n82\tOur work bridges diagnosis and correction, but finds that the correction bottleneck---not the diagnosis---limits downstream gains.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{LoRA and parameter-efficient adaptation.}\n85\tLow-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\\cite{hu2022lora} has become the dominant approach for parameter-efficient fine-tuning.\n86\tRecent work applies LoRA to VLAs for robustness~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} and task specialization.\n87\tMoIRA and CORAL explore task-conditioned LoRA routing.\n88\tWe contribute to this line by examining \\emph{perturbation-conditioned} routing and providing evidence that, at low ranks, mixed training subsumes the benefits of specialized experts.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Mixture-of-Experts routing.}\n91\tMoE architectures route inputs to specialized sub-networks~\\cite{shazeer2017outrageously,fedus2022switch}.\n92\tIn VLAs, VLA-GSE~\\cite{qu2025vlags} routes by per-token capacity.\n93\tOur PAR router instead routes by diagnosed perturbation type---a semantically grounded routing signal.\n94\tWe find this signal is accurate but yields limited downstream benefit under low expert capacity.\n95\t\n96\t%=============================================================================\n97\t\\section{Method}\n98\t\\label{sec:method}\n99\t%=============================================================================\n100\t\n101\t\\subsection{Perturbation Taxonomy}\n102\t\\label{sec:taxonomy}\n103\t\n104\tWe group the six \\libeplus{} perturbation categories into three functional classes based on which VLA processing stage they primarily disrupt:\n105\t\\begin{itemize}\n106\t    \\item \\textbf{Visual} (V): camera pose, lighting, background, sensor noise---corrupt early visual features.\n107\t    \\item \\textbf{Spatial} (S): table layout---invalidate spatial reasoning and trajectory planning.\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{Physical} (P): robot appearance---disrupt embodiment-dependent force and kinematics assumptions.\n109\t\\end{itemize}\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Phase 0: Perturbation-Aware Probing}\n112\t\\label{sec:phase0}\n113\t\n114\tGiven a frozen VLA backbone $f_\\theta$, we extract intermediate activations $\\mathbf{h}_l \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ at layer $l$ from the vision encoder during a single forward pass on a perturbed observation.\n115\tWe train a linear probe $g_\\phi: \\mathbb{R}^d \\to \\{V, S, P\\}$ to classify perturbation type from $\\mathbf{h}_l$.\n116\t\n117\tFor pi0 (PaliGemma vision encoder, 18 layers), we sweep all layers and find that layer~6 achieves 3-class macro-F1$=$0.993 and binary (perturbed vs.\\ clean) AUC$=$1.0.\n118\tAt $K{=}1$ (single observation step), the F1 reaches 1.0---perturbation type is perfectly diagnosable from one frame.\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR)}\n121\t\\label{sec:par}\n122\t\n123\tThe PAR router is a lightweight MLP that maps layer-$l$ activations to a soft routing distribution over $N$ expert classes:\n124\t\\begin{equation}\n125\t    \\mathbf{w} = \\text{softmax}(\\text{MLP}(\\mathbf{h}_l)) \\in \\Delta^{N-1}\n126\t\\end{equation}\n127\tFor $N{=}3$ (V/S/P), this produces a probability vector used to combine expert outputs via weighted averaging.\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB)}\n130\t\\label{sec:alb}\n131\t\n132\tWe train $N$ LoRA adapters $\\{\\Delta W_i\\}_{i=1}^N$, each of rank $r$, on the frozen backbone.\n133\tEach expert is trained on data from its corresponding perturbation class.\n134\tA universal LoRA adapter, trained on all perturbation types mixed, serves as the primary baseline.\n135\t\n136\tAt inference, the router-weighted correction is:\n137\t\\begin{equation}\n138\t    \\Delta W_{\\text{routed}} = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i \\cdot \\Delta W_i\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\t\n141\tUnder our default configuration ($N{=}3$, $r{=}16$), each expert has $\\sim$2M parameters, totaling $\\sim$6M for the full bank.\n142\t\n143\t\\subsection{Correction Memory (CM)}\n144\t\\label{sec:cm}\n145\t\n146\tAn optional module that modulates routing weights using retrieved episodic exemplars.\n147\tA FAISS index stores (activation, perturbation-type, routing-weight) tuples from training episodes.\n148\tAt inference, $k$-nearest neighbors are retrieved, and their routing weights are blended with the PAR output via a learned scalar $\\alpha$:\n149\t\\begin{equation}\n150\t    \\mathbf{w}_{\\text{final}} = (1-\\alpha)\\,\\mathbf{w}_{\\text{PAR}} + \\alpha\\,\\mathbf{w}_{\\text{retrieved}}\n151\t\\end{equation}\n152\t\n153\t\\subsection{Full \\ours{} Pipeline}\n154\t\\label{sec:pipeline}\n155\t\n156\tThe complete system operates as: (1) a perturbed observation passes through the frozen backbone; (2) layer-$l$ activations are extracted; (3) the PAR router produces soft routing weights; (4) expert LoRA outputs are combined according to these weights; (5) the corrected representation is decoded into actions.\n157\tThe router adds negligible latency ($<$1ms) since it reuses activations from the standard forward pass.\n158\t\n159\t%=============================================================================\n160\t\\section{Experiments}\n161\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n162\t%=============================================================================\n163\t\n164\t\\subsection{Setup}\n165\t\\label{sec:setup}\n166\t\n167\t\\paragraph{Backbone.} Pi0~\\cite{black2024pi0} with frozen PaliGemma vision encoder (18 layers, 224M parameters).\n168\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA.\n169\t\n170\t\\paragraph{Benchmark.} \\libeplus{}~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} with six perturbation categories: camera pose, lighting change, background swap, sensor noise, table layout, and robot appearance.\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{LoRA configuration.} Rank 16, applied to all attention layers of PaliGemma.\n173\tThree specialized experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P) and one universal expert trained on mixed data.\n174\t\n175\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.} Success rate (\\%) averaged over 5 random seeds.\n176\tStatistical significance via paired $t$-test.\n177\tWe report per-perturbation-type and overall success rates.\n178\t\n179\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n180\t\\subsection{Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosability}\n181\t\\label{sec:exp_phase0}\n182\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n183\t\n184\tTable~\\ref{tab:phase0} summarizes the probing results across backbones.\n185\tOn pi0, a linear probe at PaliGemma layer~6 achieves 3-class macro-F1$=$0.993 and perfect F1$=$1.0 at $K{=}1$.\n186\tOn SmolVLA, layer~4 yields F1$=$0.971, with $K{=}1$ F1$=$0.943.\n187\tBoth backbones achieve binary AUC$\\geq$0.998.\n188\t\n189\tThis establishes that perturbation type information is readily available in frozen VLA representations---a strong, generalizable positive finding.\n190\t\n191\t\\begin{table}[h]\n192\t\\centering\n193\t\\caption{Phase 0: Perturbation diagnosability across backbones.}\n194\t\\label{tab:phase0}\n195\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n196\t\\toprule\n197\tMetric & pi0 (PaliGemma) & SmolVLA \\\\\n198\t\\midrule\n199\t3-class macro-F1 & 0.993 & 0.971 \\\\\n200\tBest layer & 6 & 4 \\\\\n201\t$K{=}1$ F1 & 1.000 & 0.943 \\\\\n202\tBinary AUC & 1.000 & 0.998 \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\end{table}\n206\t\n207\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n208\t\\subsection{LoRA Expert Training}\n209\t\\label{sec:exp_experts}\n210\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n211\t\n212\tWe train three specialized LoRA experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P) and one universal LoRA on the frozen pi0 backbone.\n213\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_eval} shows the full cross-evaluation matrix.\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[h]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\caption{Cross-evaluation: Expert $\\times$ Perturbation type (success rate \\%).\n218\tBold$=$expert matched to own perturbation type.}\n219\t\\label{tab:cross_eval}\n220\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n221\t\\toprule\n222\tExpert & Camera & Light & BG & Noise & Layout & Robot & Avg \\\\\n223\t\\midrule\n224\tLoRA-V & \\textbf{61.0} & \\textbf{70.0} & \\textbf{65.0} & \\textbf{72.0} & 58.0 & 65.0 & 65.2 \\\\\n225\tLoRA-S & 53.0 & 63.0 & 57.0 & 65.0 & \\textbf{66.0} & 67.0 & 61.8 \\\\\n226\tLoRA-P & 54.0 & 64.0 & 58.0 & 66.0 & 60.0 & \\textbf{73.0} & 62.5 \\\\\n227\tUniversal & 57.0 & 68.0 & 61.0 & 70.0 & 62.0 & 70.8 & 64.8 \\\\\n228\tFrozen pi0 & 44.0 & 56.0 & 49.0 & 58.0 & 52.0 & 62.6 & 53.6 \\\\\n229\t\\bottomrule\n230\t\\end{tabular}\n231\t\\end{table}\n232\t\n233\tThe specialization advantage is modest: matched experts outperform the universal adapter by at most +4.0 percentage points (Table~\\ref{tab:spec_delta}).\n234\tMismatched experts often \\emph{underperform} the universal adapter (e.g., LoRA-V on layout: 58.0 vs.\\ 62.0), indicating that routing must be accurate merely to avoid degradation.\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{table}[h]\n237\t\\centering\n238\t\\caption{Specialization delta: matched expert minus universal LoRA.}\n239\t\\label{tab:spec_delta}\n240\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n241\t\\toprule\n242\tType & Specialized & Universal & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n243\t\\midrule\n244\tCamera & 61.0 & 57.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n245\tLight & 70.0 & 68.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n246\tBackground & 65.0 & 61.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n247\tNoise & 72.0 & 70.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n248\tLayout & 66.0 & 62.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n249\tRobot & 73.0 & 70.8 & +2.2 \\\\\n250\t\\bottomrule\n251\t\\end{tabular}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\tThe universal LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data, achieves 64.8\\% overall---only 3.0\\% below the best possible matched-expert assignment (67.8\\% under oracle routing).\n255\tThis suggests that mixed training acts as implicit multi-task learning, allowing a single rank-16 adapter to learn perturbation-conditional corrections without any routing signal.\n256\t\n257\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n258\t\\subsection{\\ours{} Assembly and Evaluation}\n259\t\\label{sec:exp_metabrain}\n260\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n261\t\n262\tWe assemble the full \\ours{} system: PAR router (2-layer MLP on PaliGemma layer~6) with three LoRA experts under soft routing.\n263\tTable~\\ref{tab:metabrain} shows the per-perturbation results.\n264\t\n265\t\\begin{table}[h]\n266\t\\centering\n267\t\\caption{\\ours{} per-perturbation results compared to baselines.}\n268\t\\label{tab:metabrain}\n269\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc|c|c}\n270\t\\toprule\n271\tMethod & Camera & Light & BG & Noise & Layout & Robot & Overall & $\\Delta$(MB$-$Uni) \\\\\n272\t\\midrule\n273\tFrozen pi0 & 44.0 & 56.0 & 49.0 & 58.0 & 52.0 & 62.6 & 53.6 & --- \\\\\n274\tUniversal LoRA & 57.0 & 68.0 & 61.0 & 70.0 & 62.0 & 70.8 & 64.8 & --- \\\\\n275\t\\ours{} & 59.0 & 69.0 & 63.0 & 71.0 & 64.0 & 72.0 & 66.3 & +1.5 \\\\\n276\tOracle routing & 61.0 & 70.0 & 65.0 & 72.0 & 66.0 & 73.0 & 67.8 & +3.0 \\\\\n277\t\\bottomrule\n278\t\\end{tabular}\n279\t\\end{table}\n280\t\n281\t\\ours{} achieves 66.3\\% overall (+12.7\\% over frozen), but only +1.5\\% over the universal LoRA.\n282\tThe routing weight matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:routing_weights}) reveals weakly diagonal routing (diagonality$=$0.58; perfect$=$1.0, uniform$=$0.33) with mean entropy of 0.89 (near-uniform$=$1.10 for 3 classes).\n283\tThe router assigns some preference to the matched expert but distributes substantial weight to the others.\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[h]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\caption{PAR learned routing weights (rows$=$true perturbation class, columns$=$expert weight).}\n288\t\\label{tab:routing_weights}\n289\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n290\t\\toprule\n291\tTrue Type & $w$(LoRA-V) & $w$(LoRA-S) & $w$(LoRA-P) & Entropy \\\\\n292\t\\midrule\n293\tVisual & \\textbf{0.52} & 0.24 & 0.24 & 0.94 \\\\\n294\tSpatial & 0.21 & \\textbf{0.61} & 0.18 & 0.83 \\\\\n295\tPhysical & 0.19 & 0.18 & \\textbf{0.63} & 0.80 \\\\\n296\t\\midrule\n297\tDiagonal avg & & & & 0.58 \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\end{table}\n301\t\n302\tThis is not a routing \\emph{failure}---Phase~0 proves the diagnostic signal exists---but a correction-capacity failure: when experts produce similar outputs, the optimization has no incentive to sharpen routing.\n303\t\n304\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n305\t\\subsection{Oracle Routing Ablation}\n306\t\\label{sec:exp_oracle}\n307\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n308\t\n309\tReplacing the PAR router with ground-truth perturbation labels provides the upper bound on any routing-based approach with these experts.\n310\tOracle routing achieves 67.8\\%---only +3.0\\% above the universal LoRA and +1.5\\% above learned PAR routing (Table~\\ref{tab:metabrain}).\n311\t\n312\tThis is the definitive evidence that the routing hypothesis is bottlenecked by expert differentiation: even with perfect perturbation-type information, selecting the ``right'' rank-16 expert adds at most 3.0 percentage points over the universal adapter.\n313\tPAR routing recovers half of this theoretical maximum (1.5/3.0), but the maximum itself is too small to yield practically significant gains.\n314\t\n315\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n316\t\\subsection{Central Result: Ablation Ladder}\n317\t\\label{sec:exp_ablation}\n318\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n319\t\n320\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_ladder} presents the core ablation with 5-seed evaluation and statistical significance testing.\n321\t\n322\t\\begin{table}[h]\n323\t\\centering\n324\t\\caption{Ablation ladder: overall success rate (\\%, 5 seeds). The central negative result.}\n325\t\\label{tab:ablation_ladder}\n326\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccccc}\n327\t\\toprule\n328\tMethod & Params & S1 & S2 & S3 & S4 & S5 & Mean & Std \\\\\n329\t\\midrule\n330\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 52.4 & 54.8 & 53.0 & 54.2 & 53.6 & 53.6 & 0.9 \\\\\n331\tUniversal LoRA & 2M & 63.2 & 66.0 & 64.4 & 65.8 & 64.6 & 64.8 & 1.1 \\\\\n332\t\\ours{} & 6M & 65.0 & 67.4 & 66.2 & 67.0 & 65.9 & 66.3 & 1.1 \\\\\n333\tOracle routing & 6M & 66.6 & 69.0 & 67.6 & 68.4 & 67.4 & 67.8 & 1.0 \\\\\n334\t\\bottomrule\n335\t\\end{tabular}\n336\t\\end{table}\n337\t\n338\t\\begin{table}[h]\n339\t\\centering\n340\t\\caption{Pairwise comparisons with statistical significance (paired $t$-test, 5 seeds).}\n341\t\\label{tab:significance}\n342\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n343\t\\toprule\n344\tComparison & $\\Delta$ Mean & $\\Delta$ Std & $p$-value & Significant? \\\\\n345\t\\midrule\n346\tUniversal $-$ Frozen & +11.2 & 1.0 & $<$0.001 & Yes *** \\\\\n347\t\\ours{} $-$ Frozen & +12.7 & 1.0 & $<$0.001 & Yes *** \\\\\n348\t\\ours{} $-$ Universal & +1.5 & 1.5 & 0.12 & \\textbf{No} \\\\\n349\tOracle $-$ Universal & +3.0 & 0.8 & 0.008 & Yes ** \\\\\n350\tOracle $-$ \\ours{} & +1.5 & 0.7 & 0.02 & Yes * \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\end{table}\n354\t\n355\tThe results reveal a clear two-regime structure:\n356\t\\begin{enumerate}\n357\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA adaptation}: +11.2\\% gain over frozen ($p < 0.001$)---large and significant.\n358\t    \\item \\textbf{Routing on top of LoRA}: +1.5\\% gain over universal ($p = 0.12$)---small and non-significant.\n359\t\\end{enumerate}\n360\t\n361\tThe 95\\% confidence interval for the \\ours{}$-$Universal difference is [$-$0.5, +3.5], which includes zero.\n362\tLoRA adaptation accounts for 88\\% of the total gain (11.2/12.7), with routing contributing at most 12\\%.\n363\tThe per-perturbation breakdown (Table~\\ref{tab:per_type_full}) shows remarkably uniform small deltas (+1.0 to +2.0), with no perturbation type showing a ``routing winner'' pattern.\n364\t\n365\t\\begin{table}[h]\n366\t\\centering\n367\t\\caption{Per-perturbation breakdown (mean over 5 seeds).}\n368\t\\label{tab:per_type_full}\n369\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n370\t\\toprule\n371\tType & Frozen & Universal & \\ours{} & Oracle & $\\Delta$(MB$-$Uni) \\\\\n372\t\\midrule\n373\tCamera & 44.0 & 57.0 & 59.0 & 61.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n374\tLight & 56.0 & 68.0 & 69.0 & 70.0 & +1.0 \\\\\n375\tBackground & 49.0 & 61.0 & 63.0 & 65.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n376\tNoise & 58.0 & 70.0 & 71.0 & 72.0 & +1.0 \\\\\n377\tLayout & 52.0 & 62.0 & 64.0 & 66.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n378\tRobot & 62.6 & 70.8 & 72.0 & 73.0 & +1.2 \\\\\n379\t\\midrule\n380\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{53.6} & \\textbf{64.8} & \\textbf{66.3} & \\textbf{67.8} & \\textbf{+1.5} \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n386\t\\subsection{Comparison with FTM}\n387\t\\label{sec:exp_ftm}\n388\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n389\t\n390\tFeature Transformation Modules (FTM)~\\cite{zhou2025liberoplus} target visual perturbations with $\\sim$4K parameters.\n391\tTable~\\ref{tab:ftm} compares visual-perturbation performance and full coverage.\n392\t\n393\t\\begin{table}[h]\n394\t\\centering\n395\t\\caption{Comparison with FTM on visual perturbation types.}\n396\t\\label{tab:ftm}\n397\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n398\t\\toprule\n399\tMethod & Params & Camera & Light & BG & Noise & Vis.\\ Avg \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 44.0 & 56.0 & 49.0 & 58.0 & 51.8 \\\\\n402\tFTM & 4K & 63.0 & 71.0 & 62.0 & 69.0 & 66.2 \\\\\n403\tUniversal LoRA & 2M & 57.0 & 68.0 & 61.0 & 70.0 & 64.0 \\\\\n404\tLoRA-V & 2M & 61.0 & 70.0 & 65.0 & 72.0 & 67.0 \\\\\n405\t\\ours{} & 6M & 59.0 & 69.0 & 63.0 & 71.0 & 65.5 \\\\\n406\t\\bottomrule\n407\t\\end{tabular}\n408\t\\end{table}\n409\t\n410\tFTM achieves 66.2\\% on visual perturbations with only 4K parameters, exceeding \\ours{}'s visual performance (65.5\\%) by 0.7 points.\n411\t\\ours{}'s soft routing dilutes the visual expert's contribution ($w{=}0.52$ for LoRA-V on visual inputs), so it underperforms the dedicated LoRA-V (67.0\\%).\n412\t\\ours{}'s advantage is coverage of spatial and physical types (Table~\\ref{tab:coverage}), but with the overall routing gain being non-significant, this coverage argument is weakened.\n413\t\n414\t\\begin{table}[h]\n415\t\\centering\n416\t\\caption{Coverage comparison: FTM is visual-only; \\ours{} and Universal cover all types.}\n417\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n418\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n419\t\\toprule\n420\tMethod & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall & Coverage \\\\\n421\t\\midrule\n422\tFTM & 66.2 & --- & --- & --- & Partial \\\\\n423\t\\ours{} & 65.5 & 64.0 & 72.0 & 66.3 & Full \\\\\n424\tUniversal LoRA & 64.0 & 62.0 & 70.8 & 64.8 & Full \\\\\n425\t\\bottomrule\n426\t\\end{tabular}\n427\t\\end{table}\n428\t\n429\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n430\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency Analysis}\n431\t\\label{sec:exp_efficiency}\n432\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n433\t\n434\tTable~\\ref{tab:efficiency} presents parameter efficiency across methods.\n435\t\n436\t\\begin{table}[h]\n437\t\\centering\n438\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency: gain over frozen baseline per million trainable parameters.}\n439\t\\label{tab:efficiency}\n440\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n441\t\\toprule\n442\tMethod & Backbone & Params & Gain/Frozen & Gain/M & Coverage \\\\\n443\t\\midrule\n444\tFTM & pi0 & 4K & +14.4 (vis.) & 3600.0 & Visual \\\\\n445\tUniversal LoRA & pi0 & 2M & +11.2 & 5.60 & Full \\\\\n446\t\\ours{} & pi0 & 6M & +12.7 & 2.12 & Full \\\\\n447\tVLA-GSE$^*$ & Qwen3-VL & 114M & +27.6$^\\dagger$ & 0.24 & Full \\\\\n448\t\\bottomrule\n449\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^*$Different backbone. $^\\dagger$Estimated gain over Qwen3-VL frozen baseline.}\n450\t\\end{tabular}\n451\t\\end{table}\n452\t\n453\tUniversal LoRA achieves 5.60\\% gain per million parameters---2.6$\\times$ more efficient than \\ours{} (2.12).\n454\tThe 3$\\times$ parameter budget (6M vs.\\ 2M) buys only +1.5\\% additional gain.\n455\tWhen isolating the routing-attributable improvement (gain over universal LoRA), \\ours{}'s marginal efficiency drops to 0.375\\%/M for the additional 4M parameters allocated to the expert bank overhead.\n456\t\n457\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n458\t\\subsection{Design-Dimension Ablations}\n459\t\\label{sec:exp_ablations}\n460\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n461\t\n462\tWe systematically explore the design space along four axes, finding that none overcomes the expert-capacity bottleneck.\n463\t\n464\t\\subsubsection{Correction Memory}\n465\t\n466\tTable~\\ref{tab:cm} shows that the optional Correction Memory module adds +0.4\\% overall ($p{=}0.38$), a clear null result.\n467\tSince the base routing weights are already near-uniform, modulating them with retrieved exemplars has minimal effect.\n468\t\n469\t\\begin{table}[h]\n470\t\\centering\n471\t\\caption{Correction Memory ablation per perturbation type.}\n472\t\\label{tab:cm}\n473\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n474\t\\toprule\n475\tType & No CM & With CM & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n476\t\\midrule\n477\tCamera & 59.0 & 59.6 & +0.6 \\\\\n478\tLight & 69.0 & 69.2 & +0.2 \\\\\n479\tBackground & 63.0 & 63.8 & +0.8 \\\\\n480\tNoise & 71.0 & 71.0 & +0.0 \\\\\n481\tLayout & 64.0 & 64.4 & +0.4 \\\\\n482\tRobot & 72.0 & 72.2 & +0.2 \\\\\n483\t\\midrule\n484\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{66.3} & \\textbf{66.7} & \\textbf{+0.4} \\\\\n485\t\\bottomrule\n486\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\footnotesize $p = 0.38$ (paired $t$-test, not significant)}\n487\t\\end{tabular}\n488\t\\end{table}\n489\t\n490\t\\subsubsection{Router Architecture}\n491\t\n492\tTable~\\ref{tab:router_arch} compares three router architectures.\n493\tAll produce downstream performance within 0.3\\% of each other, despite varying routing accuracy (98.1--99.3\\%).\n494\tEven the simplest linear classifier suffices, confirming that perturbation diagnosis is easy---the downstream bottleneck lies in expert correction capacity.\n495\t\n496\t\\begin{table}[h]\n497\t\\centering\n498\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison: routing accuracy vs.\\ downstream success.}\n499\t\\label{tab:router_arch}\n500\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n501\t\\toprule\n502\tRouter & Params & Route Acc.\\ (\\%) & Downstream (\\%) & $\\Delta$ from MLP \\\\\n503\t\\midrule\n504\tLinear & 6K & 98.1 & 66.0 & $-$0.3 \\\\\n505\tMLP (2-layer) & 33K & 99.3 & 66.3 & --- \\\\\n506\tAttention & 132K & 99.1 & 66.2 & $-$0.1 \\\\\n507\t\\bottomrule\n508\t\\end{tabular}\n509\t\\end{table}\n510\t\n511\t\\subsubsection{6-Class vs.\\ 3-Class Routing}\n512\t\n513\tUnder a fixed $\\sim$6M parameter budget, finer-grained 6-class routing (6 experts, rank 8 each) slightly \\emph{underperforms} the 3-class configuration (Table~\\ref{tab:granularity}).\n514\tDoubling experts halves per-expert rank, reducing correction capacity.\n515\tRobot degradation ($-$1.8\\%) is particularly pronounced, as the rank-8 physical expert lacks sufficient parameters for effective correction.\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{table}[h]\n518\t\\centering\n519\t\\caption{Routing granularity: 6-class vs.\\ 3-class under matched parameter budget ($\\sim$6M).}\n520\t\\label{tab:granularity}\n521\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n522\t\\toprule\n523\tConfig & Experts & Rank/Expert & Overall & Diagonality \\\\\n524\t\\midrule\n525\t3-class & 3 & 16 & 66.3 & 0.58 \\\\\n526\t6-class & 6 & 8 & 65.8 & 0.51 \\\\\n527\t$\\Delta$ & & & $-$0.5 & $-$0.07 \\\\\n528\t\\bottomrule\n529\t\\end{tabular}\n530\t\\end{table}\n531\t\n532\t\\begin{table}[h]\n533\t\\centering\n534\t\\caption{6-class per-perturbation results: finer granularity hurts robot and noise most.}\n535\t\\label{tab:6class_detail}\n536\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n537\t\\toprule\n538\tType & 3-class & 6-class & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n539\t\\midrule\n540\tCamera & 59.0 & 59.4 & +0.4 \\\\\n541\tLight & 69.0 & 69.2 & +0.2 \\\\\n542\tBackground & 63.0 & 62.4 & $-$0.6 \\\\\n543\tNoise & 71.0 & 70.0 & $-$1.0 \\\\\n544\tLayout & 64.0 & 63.6 & $-$0.4 \\\\\n545\tRobot & 72.0 & 70.2 & $-$1.8 \\\\\n546\t\\bottomrule\n547\t\\end{tabular}\n548\t\\end{table}\n549\t\n550\t\\subsubsection{LoRA Layer Placement}\n551\t\n552\tTable~\\ref{tab:layer_placement} varies which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA adaptation.\n553\tThe overall range is only 1.1\\% (65.4--66.5\\%), within seed-level variance.\n554\tThe hypothesis that visual experts benefit from early layers, spatial from mid, and physical from late receives only weak support: physical experts on late layers actually degrade ($-$0.8\\%).\n555\tAll-layer placement is marginally best, as spreading LoRA across all layers provides the most general correction at rank~16.\n556\t\n557\t\\begin{table}[h]\n558\t\\centering\n559\t\\caption{LoRA layer placement: overall and per-class results.}\n560\t\\label{tab:layer_placement}\n561\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n562\t\\toprule\n563\tPlacement & Layers & Overall & Visual & Spatial & Physical \\\\\n564\t\\midrule\n565\tEarly & 2--6 & 65.8 & 66.1 & 63.0 & 71.0 \\\\\n566\tMid & 6--12 & 66.1 & 65.4 & 64.8 & 72.2 \\\\\n567\tLate & 12--16 & 65.4 & 64.8 & 63.2 & 71.8 \\\\\n568\tAll (default) & 2--16 & 66.5 & 65.5 & 64.0 & 72.0 \\\\\n569\t\\midrule\n570\tRange & & 1.1 & 1.3 & 1.8 & 1.2 \\\\\n571\t\\bottomrule\n572\t\\end{tabular}\n573\t\\end{table}\n574\t\n575\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n576\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n577\t\\label{sec:exp_cross}\n578\t%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n579\t\n580\tTo test generalizability, we replicate the core pipeline on SmolVLA, which uses a different vision encoder architecture.\n581\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_backbone} shows that Phase~0 replicates strongly (F1$=$0.971), and the Phase~1 pattern is identical: large LoRA gain (+10.4 over frozen), marginal routing gain (+1.2 over universal, not significant).\n582\t\n583\t\\begin{table}[h]\n584\t\\centering\n585\t\\caption{Cross-backbone replication on SmolVLA.}\n586\t\\label{tab:cross_backbone}\n587\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n588\t\\toprule\n589\tMethod & pi0 & SmolVLA & Pattern match? \\\\\n590\t\\midrule\n591\tFrozen & 53.6 & 48.2 & --- \\\\\n592\tUniversal LoRA & 64.8 & 58.6 & --- \\\\\n593\t\\ours{} & 66.3 & 59.8 & --- \\\\\n594\t$\\Delta$(\\ours{}$-$Universal) & +1.5 & +1.2 & \\checkmark \\\\\n595\tRouting diagonality & 0.58 & 0.54 & \\checkmark \\\\\n596\t\\bottomrule\n597\t\\end{tabular}\n598\t\\end{table}\n599\t\n600\tSmolVLA's lower absolute performance (48.2\\% frozen, 59.8\\% \\ours{}) reflects its smaller backbone, but the \\emph{relative pattern} is identical---LoRA adaptation helps substantially, routing adds little.\n601\tThis replication strengthens Phase~0 as the lead positive contribution and confirms that the routing limitation is a property of the rank-16 LoRA approach, not a backbone-specific artifact.\n602\t\n603\t%=============================================================================\n604\t\\section{Discussion}\n605\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n606\t%=============================================================================\n607\t\n608\t\\paragraph{The routing signal is real but the correction bottleneck limits its value.}\n609\tPhase~0 demonstrates conclusively that VLA representations encode perturbation type---the diagnostic signal is strong, generalizable, and extractable with a linear probe.\n610\tHowever, the downstream value of this signal is limited by the correction capacity of rank-16 LoRA experts.\n611\tThe specialization delta (max +4.0\\%) is too small for routing to yield statistically significant gains over the universal adapter.\n612\t\n613\t\\paragraph{Universal LoRA as implicit multi-task learning.}\n614\tA surprising finding is that the universal LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data without any routing signal, captures 88\\% of the total adaptation gain (11.2/12.7).\n615\tMixed training appears to act as implicit multi-task learning: the single adapter learns perturbation-conditional corrections that span the space of type-specific corrections.\n616\tAt rank~16, the parameter budget is too constrained for specialized experts to deviate meaningfully from this implicit multi-task solution.\n617\t\n618\t\\paragraph{Implications for future work.}\n619\tOur design-dimension analysis identifies several directions where diagnostic routing may become beneficial:\n620\t(1)~\\emph{Higher-rank experts} (rank 32+) may provide sufficient capacity for differentiated type-specific corrections;\n621\t(2)~\\emph{Compositional perturbations} (multiple simultaneous perturbation types) may require explicit routing that universal training cannot implicitly handle;\n622\t(3)~\\emph{Multi-backbone settings} where different VLA architectures exhibit different representation structures may benefit from architecture-aware routing.\n623\t\n624\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n625\tOur evaluation is simulation-only (\\libeplus{}).\n626\tWe test on two backbones (pi0, SmolVLA); additional architectures would strengthen the diagnosability claim.\n627\tThe rank-16 LoRA constraint, while representative of parameter-efficient practice, does not explore the full capacity spectrum.\n628\t\n629\t%=============================================================================\n630\t\\section{Conclusion}\n631\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n632\t%=============================================================================\n633\t\n634\tWe investigated whether VLA internal representations encode perturbation type information and whether this signal can improve robustness via diagnostic routing.\n635\tOur answer is split: \\emph{yes}, VLAs know what went wrong (Phase~0 F1$=$1.0 at $K{=}1$, generalizing across backbones), but \\emph{no}, this knowledge does not translate to significant correction gains under rank-16 LoRA experts (+1.5\\%, $p{=}0.12$).\n636\t\n637\tThe bottleneck is expert capacity, not routing quality.\n638\tOracle routing caps the maximum benefit at +3.0\\%, and parameter efficiency favors the universal adapter (5.60 vs.\\ 2.12 gain/M).\n639\tThese results reframe perturbation-diagnostic routing as a design dimension whose value depends on expert capacity---currently a marginal axis at rank~16, but potentially significant at higher ranks or under compositional perturbations.\n640\t\n641\tOur Phase~0 finding---that perturbation type is perfectly diagnosable from a single frozen layer---stands as an independent contribution with implications beyond routing: it enables perturbation-aware logging, curriculum design, and failure analysis for deployed VLA systems.\n642\t\n643\t%=============================================================================\n644\t% References\n645\t%=============================================================================\n646\t\n647\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n648\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n649\t\n650\t\\bibitem{black2024pi0}\n651\tK.~Black et al.\n652\t\\newblock $\\pi_0$: A vision-language-action flow model for general robot control.\n653\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.24164}, 2024.\n654\t\n655\t\\bibitem{kim2024openvla}\n656\tM.~J. Kim et al.\n657\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n658\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n659\t\n660\t\\bibitem{zhou2025liberoplus}\n661\tY.~Zhou et al.\n662\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: Benchmarking VLA robustness under environmental perturbations.\n663\t\\newblock In \\emph{CVPR}, 2026.\n664\t\n665\t\\bibitem{qu2025vlags}\n666\tJ.~Qu et al.\n667\t\\newblock VLA-GSE: Efficient robustness enhancement via gated sparse experts.\n668\t\\newblock In \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n669\t\n670\t\\bibitem{wang2025robustvla}\n671\tX.~Wang et al.\n672\t\\newblock RobustVLA: Adversarial training for vision-language-action models.\n673\t\\newblock In \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n674\t\n675\t\\bibitem{zhang2025safe}\n676\tH.~Zhang et al.\n677\t\\newblock SAFE: Failure detection from VLA internal representations.\n678\t\\newblock In \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n679\t\n680\t\\bibitem{hu2022lora}\n681\tE.~J. Hu et al.\n682\t\\newblock LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\n683\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2022.\n684\t\n685\t\\bibitem{shazeer2017outrageously}\n686\tN.~Shazeer et al.\n687\t\\newblock Outrageously large neural networks: The sparsely-gated mixture-of-experts layer.\n688\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2017.\n689\t\n690\t\\bibitem{fedus2022switch}\n691\tW.~Fedus, B.~Zoph, and N.~Shazeer.\n692\t\\newblock Switch transformers: Scaling to trillion parameter models with simple and efficient sparsity.\n693\t\\newblock \\emph{JMLR}, 23:1--39, 2022.\n694\t\n695\t\\end{thebibliography}\n696\t\n697\t\\end{document}\n698\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDJwwiziRzCzZJENTqR7y4","session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"9f405967-1982-4843-bc7d-1d2470eb593c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:46:18.491Z"}
