{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgS46ueuGs5A8Jd8wKpTUy","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is the summary:\n\n**Review for \"Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong?\" (worst-tier dream, CoRL 2026)**\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (Borderline, leaning reject)**\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Phase 0 (F1=1.0 at K=1, cross-backbone replication on SmolVLA) is a clean positive finding\n- Honest reporting of the negative Phase 1 result with proper 5-seed statistical testing (p=0.12, CI spanning zero)\n- Oracle routing ablation at 67.8% definitively caps routing benefit at +3.0%, shifting diagnosis from \"bad router\" to \"insufficient expert differentiation\"\n- Design-dimension ablations (4 axes) systematically rule out alternative explanations\n- Cross-backbone replication confirms the negative pattern is structural, not backbone-specific\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n- The central claim (expert capacity is the bottleneck) is never experimentally validated -- no rank sweep (r=16/32/64)\n- Parameter-mismatched primary comparison: Meta-Brain (6M) vs. Universal LoRA (2M). A rank-48 universal (~6M) is missing\n- No random routing baseline to separate routing accuracy from expert ensembling\n- Training data imbalance across experts is uncontrolled\n- Bibliography critically thin (9 references, missing MoE-LoRA literature, probing foundations, TTA methods)\n\n**Path to acceptance:** Run (1) rank sweep to validate capacity-bottleneck thesis, (2) parameter-matched universal LoRA, (3) random routing baseline. If specialization delta grows with rank, the narrative becomes actionable (\"routing unlocks at higher capacity\") rather than purely negative.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6d0104c58b412137 (use SendMessage with to: 'a6d0104c58b412137' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 116095\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 189713</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"46ea5bf5-8180-4ea8-9d2d-a4ea38a6eb33","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:48:30.902Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Standards\n- CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), ~39% acceptance rate\n- OpenReview 1-10 scale: 10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below, 3=clear reject\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires robot learning relevance; evaluation in simulation or real robots\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models internally encode perturbation type information, and whether this can be exploited for targeted correction. Two main findings:\n1. **Phase 0 (Positive)**: Frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=1.0 at K=1 on pi0, F1=0.971 on SmolVLA). This generalizes across architectures.\n2. **Phase 1 (Negative)**: Perturbation-diagnostic routing (Meta-Brain) with type-specialized LoRA experts yields only +1.5% over universal LoRA (p=0.12, not significant). Oracle routing caps benefit at +3.0%. The bottleneck is expert capacity at rank 16, not routing quality.\n\nArchitecture: PAR router (MLP on layer 6 activations) → soft-weighted combination of 3 specialized LoRA experts (V/S/P, rank 16 each, ~6M total). Also includes optional Correction Memory module (FAISS-based).\n\nBenchmark: LIBERO-Plus (6 perturbation categories), evaluated with 5 seeds, paired t-tests.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\nThe review_context.yaml lists 10 imagined experiments:\n1. LoRA expert training (3 specialized + 1 universal) — cross-evaluation matrix\n2. Meta-Brain assembly (PAR router + LoRA bank) — end-to-end evaluation\n3. Oracle routing ablation — upper bound\n4. Ablation ladder (5-seed, statistical tests) — central evidence\n5. FTM reproduction (visual-only baseline comparison)\n6. Parameter efficiency analysis\n7. Correction Memory ablation\n8. Router architecture comparison (Linear/MLP/Attention)\n9. 6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\n10. Cross-backbone validation (SmolVLA)\n11. LoRA layer placement ablation\n\n## What the Paper Actually Reports (Tables/Sections)\n- Table 1: Phase 0 probing results (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\n- Table 2: Cross-evaluation matrix (Expert × Perturbation)\n- Table 3: Specialization deltas\n- Table 4: Meta-Brain per-perturbation results\n- Table 5: PAR routing weight matrix\n- Table 6: Ablation ladder (5 seeds)\n- Table 7: Pairwise significance tests\n- Table 8: Per-perturbation breakdown\n- Table 9: FTM comparison (visual)\n- Table 10: Coverage comparison\n- Table 11: Parameter efficiency\n- Table 12: Correction Memory ablation\n- Table 13: Router architecture comparison\n- Table 14: 6-class vs 3-class\n- Table 15: 6-class per-perturbation\n- Table 16: LoRA layer placement\n- Table 17: Cross-backbone (SmolVLA)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focused on methods/experiments)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focused on methods/experiments)\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Methodological Concerns\n### 6. Scores (provide numerical scores 1-10 for each):\n   - Soundness (technical correctness of methods)\n   - Empirical Rigor (experimental design, statistical validity)\n   - Reproducibility\n   - Overall Score (1-10 on OpenReview scale)\n   - Confidence (1-5, where 5=very confident)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a paper that honestly reports a negative result — evaluate whether the negative result is well-established and informative. Consider the coverage of planned experiments vs. what's actually in the paper.","agentId":"a6d0104c58b412137","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is the summary:\n\n**Review for \"Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong?\" (worst-tier dream, CoRL 2026)**\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (Borderline, leaning reject)**\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Phase 0 (F1=1.0 at K=1, cross-backbone replication on SmolVLA) is a clean positive finding\n- Honest reporting of the negative Phase 1 result with proper 5-seed statistical testing (p=0.12, CI spanning zero)\n- Oracle routing ablation at 67.8% definitively caps routing benefit at +3.0%, shifting diagnosis from \"bad router\" to \"insufficient expert differentiation\"\n- Design-dimension ablations (4 axes) systematically rule out alternative explanations\n- Cross-backbone replication confirms the negative pattern is structural, not backbone-specific\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n- The central claim (expert capacity is the bottleneck) is never experimentally validated -- no rank sweep (r=16/32/64)\n- Parameter-mismatched primary comparison: Meta-Brain (6M) vs. Universal LoRA (2M). A rank-48 universal (~6M) is missing\n- No random routing baseline to separate routing accuracy from expert ensembling\n- Training data imbalance across experts is uncontrolled\n- Bibliography critically thin (9 references, missing MoE-LoRA literature, probing foundations, TTA methods)\n\n**Path to acceptance:** Run (1) rank sweep to validate capacity-bottleneck thesis, (2) parameter-matched universal LoRA, (3) random routing baseline. If specialization delta grows with rank, the narrative becomes actionable (\"routing unlocks at higher capacity\") rather than purely negative.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/