{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDJwwiziRzCzZJENTqR7y4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Paper reviewed:** \"Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing\" (worst-case scenario for CoRL 2026)\n\n**Overall score: 4/10 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Honest framing of a negative result with clear statistical reporting (5-seed, paired t-tests, CIs)\n- Phase 0 cross-backbone replication (F1 > 0.97 on both pi0 and SmolVLA) is a genuine generalization result\n- Oracle ablation cleanly identifies expert capacity (not routing quality) as the bottleneck\n- Routing-accuracy-to-downstream dissociation (Table 8) provides a useful falsification datapoint\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n1. **Novelty:** Phase 0 is an incremental extension of SAFE/OpenVLA probing. The routing architecture (h' = h + sum w_k * LoRA_k(h)) is identical to existing MoE-LoRA methods (LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA). The negative result may be predictable from low-rank adaptation literature.\n2. **Bibliography:** 9 references — critically below CoRL norms of 30-50. Entire directly-relevant literatures missing: MoE-LoRA, test-time adaptation, probing foundations, adapter composition. Three works named in text but absent from bibliography.\n3. **Missing controls:** No parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48, ~6M), no random routing baseline, no rank-scaling experiments to validate the \"capacity bottleneck\" narrative.\n4. **Inflated contributions:** Three claimed contributions are actually one finding with setup and ablations.\n\n**Path to resubmission:** Run rank-scaling experiments (32/64/128), add parameter-matched baselines, expand bibliography to 30+, engage with MoE-LoRA literature, reframe as a single contribution.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/worst/review_report.md`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: add608f173ee8d7b8 (use SendMessage with to: 'add608f173ee8d7b8' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 110472\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 197976</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f290d644-682c-40ce-bbe2-cd72e060667b","uuid":"a9114e11-2450-4012-b8d5-0d4735520fa0","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:49:01.199Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Venue Standards\n- CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), ~39% acceptance rate\n- OpenReview 1-10 scale: 10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below, 3=clear reject\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL requires robot learning relevance; evaluation in simulation or real robots\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether VLA internal representations encode perturbation type, and whether this can be exploited via diagnostic routing. Two findings:\n1. **Phase 0**: Linear probes on frozen VLA activations achieve near-perfect perturbation type classification (F1=1.0 on pi0 layer 6 at K=1, F1=0.971 on SmolVLA layer 4). This is presented as a general property of VLA representations.\n2. **Phase 1**: Meta-Brain (PAR router + 3 specialized LoRA experts, rank 16, 6M params) achieves 66.3% on LIBERO-Plus, only +1.5% over universal LoRA (64.8%), not statistically significant (p=0.12). Oracle routing caps benefit at +3.0%. Bottleneck is expert capacity, not routing quality.\n\nThree claimed contributions:\n1. Perturbation diagnosability as a general VLA property\n2. Informative negative result on routing\n3. Design-dimension analysis mapping the landscape\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper (Section 2)\n- **VLA robustness**: LIBERO-Plus/FTM [3], VLA-GSE [4] (MoE routing, 114M params, Qwen3-VL, 81.2%), RobustVLA [5] (adversarial training), SAFE [6] (failure detection from internal representations)\n- **LoRA/PEFT**: LoRA [7], MoIRA and CORAL mentioned but not cited with numbers\n- **MoE routing**: Shazeer et al. [8], Switch Transformers [9]\n- Only 9 references total\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe planned experiments included 10 experiments covering: expert training, Meta-Brain assembly, oracle ablation, ablation ladder, FTM comparison, parameter efficiency, Correction Memory, router architecture, 6-class vs 3-class, cross-backbone (SmolVLA), layer placement. The paper covers all of them.\n\n## Key Numbers\n- Frozen pi0: 53.6%, Universal LoRA (2M): 64.8%, Meta-Brain (6M): 66.3%, Oracle (6M): 67.8%\n- Meta-Brain−Universal: +1.5%, p=0.12, 95% CI [−0.5, +3.5]\n- FTM (4K params): 66.2% on visual types, beating Meta-Brain's 65.5% on visual\n- Cross-backbone: SmolVLA replicates Phase 0 (F1=0.971) and Phase 1 pattern (Δ=+1.2, not significant)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focused on novelty and positioning)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focused on novelty and related work gaps)\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n### 5. Related Work Gaps and Positioning Issues\n### 6. Scores (provide numerical scores 1-10 for each):\n   - Novelty/Originality\n   - Significance/Impact\n   - Related Work Completeness\n   - Clarity of Presentation\n   - Overall Score (1-10 on OpenReview scale)\n   - Confidence (1-5, where 5=very confident)\n\nBe rigorous. Key questions to consider:\n- Is probing frozen representations for perturbation type truly novel? (SAFE [6] already detects perturbations from internal representations)\n- Is the negative result on routing surprising or predictable given the MoE/LoRA literature?\n- Only 9 references — is this sufficient for a venue paper?\n- Is the \"design-dimension analysis\" a real contribution or just a collection of ablations?\n- How does this compare to the broader probing literature (probing classifiers in NLP, vision)?\n- Does the paper adequately position against VLA-GSE which achieves 81.2% with routing?\n- Is the framing as \"informative negative result\" sufficient for a CoRL accept?","agentId":"add608f173ee8d7b8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Paper reviewed:** \"Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing\" (worst-case scenario for CoRL 2026)\n\n**Overall score: 4/10 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Honest framing of a negative result with clear statistical reporting (5-seed, paired t-tests, CIs)\n- Phase 0 cross-backbone replication (F1 > 0.97 on both pi0 and SmolVLA) is a genuine generalization result\n- Oracle ablation cleanly identifies expert capacity (not routing quality) as the bottleneck\n- Routing-accuracy-to-downstream dissociation (Table 8) provides a useful falsification datapoint\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n1. **Novelty:** Phase 0 is an incremental extension of SAFE/OpenVLA probing. The routing architecture (h' = h + sum w_k * LoRA_k(h)) is identical to existing MoE-LoRA methods (LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA). The negative result may be predictable from low-rank adaptation literature.\n2. **Bibliography:** 9 references — critically below CoRL norms of 30-50. Entire directly-relevant literatures missing: MoE-LoRA, test-time adaptation, probing foundations, adapter composition. Three works named in text but absent from bibliography.\n3. **Missing controls:** No parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48, ~6M), no random routing baseline, no rank-scaling experiments to validate the \"capacity bottleneck\" narrative.\n4. **Inflated contributions:** Three claimed contributions are actually one finding with setup and ablations.\n\n**Path to resubmission:** Run rank-scaling experiments (32/64/128), add parameter-matched baselines, expand bibliography to 30+, engage with MoE-LoRA literature, reframe as a single contribution.\n\nReport saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/worst/review_report.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":197976,"totalTokens":110472,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5336,"cache_read_input_tokens":104599,"output_tokens":536,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5336},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":536,"cache_read_input_tokens":104599,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5336,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5336,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
